window.MOLASTRIA_QUASI = {"index":{"title":"Quasiparticles","sub":"The “elementary particles” of solids: phonons, holes, magnons, excitons, polarons, plasmons, polaritons, Cooper pairs and anyons","eyebrow":"MOLASTRIA · QUASIPARTICLES","h1":"Quasiparticles","question":"Why do 10²³ strongly interacting electrons and nuclei so often behave like a small crowd of nearly free particles?","lead":"The low-energy excitations of a solid can often be written as a crowd of nearly independent “particles” with definite energy and momentum, mass, charge and spin, which collide, condense, and are created and absorbed in discrete portions. They exist only inside materials and are called <b>quasiparticles</b>. Some are dressed electrons (holes, Fermi-liquid quasiparticles, polarons), some are collective excitations in which many particles move together (phonons, magnons, plasmons), some are mixtures of light and matter (polaritons), and some are anyons carrying fractional charge and picking up arbitrary phases on exchange. This section explains them one by one in twelve chapters: what each is, why it exists, how it is measured and where research stands, each chapter with a demo computed live in your browser. Phonons also have a nine-chapter <a href=\"../quasiparticles/phonons/\">Phonon hall</a> of their own.","scope":"<b>How to read the labels.</b> Every chapter and every key point carries a status: history, theorem (derived rigorously from quantum or statistical mechanics), established by experiment, approximation (valid under the stated conditions), frontier and open. Demos carry one of three labels: exact (computed live from the model’s definition), toy model (a minimal model built to show one thing) and data (numbers from the literature). Material parameters are common textbook and literature values, for orders of magnitude only.","levels":{"history":["History","Documented history and facts about the literature"],"proved":["Theorem","Derived rigorously from quantum or statistical mechanics"],"tested":["Established","Supported by several independent experiments or calculations, and in everyday use"],"approx":["Approximation","Holds under the stated conditions and fails outside them"],"frontier":["Frontier","Developing fast; conclusions and best numbers are still changing"],"open":["Open","Not yet achieved or explained by anyone"]},"demoKinds":{"exact":["Exact","Computed live from the model’s definition (the model is described under each demo)"],"toy":["Toy model","A minimal model built to show one thing clearly; its parameters do not correspond to a real material"],"data":["Data","Numbers from textbooks or papers; the demo only computes and plots"]},"groups":[{"title":"What a quasiparticle is","ids":["what"]},{"title":"Collective excitations","ids":["phonons","magnons","plasmons"]},{"title":"Dressed electrons","ids":["holes","fermi","polarons"]},{"title":"Bound states and mixtures","ids":["excitons","polaritons","cooper"]},{"title":"Topology and measurement","ids":["anyons","measure"]}],"chapters":[{"id":"what","n":"01","title":"What a quasiparticle is","summary":"A piece of solid holds 10²³ electrons and nuclei, all interacting strongly. Yet its low-energy excitations often behave like a crowd of nearly independent “particles” with definite energy and momentum, mass, charge and spin, which can even collide. Such particles exist only inside the material and are called quasiparticles. This chapter explains what the idea really means and when it holds: look for a sharp peak in the spectral function.","level":"approx","demos":["spectral","zoo"]},{"id":"phonons","n":"02","title":"Phonons: the first and cleanest quasiparticle","summary":"Count the lattice vibrations in quantum-mechanical portions and each portion is a phonon. It is the prototype of the “collective excitation” kind of quasiparticle: a boson whose number is not conserved, carrying crystal momentum rather than mechanical momentum, and responsible for heat capacity, thermal conduction and thermal expansion. This chapter is an overview; the details (nine chapters, eleven demos) are in the Phonon hall.","level":"proved","demos":["count"]},{"id":"holes","n":"03","title":"Holes and effective mass: band electrons are already quasiparticles","summary":"Electrons in a crystal feel a periodic potential, and their energy becomes a band E(k). Their response to forces is set by the curvature of the band: the effective mass can be ten times lighter than a free electron, or negative. In an almost full band the few missing electrons together behave like particles of positive charge and positive mass: holes. The “anomalous” sign of the Hall effect, bipolar conduction in semiconductors and the massless Dirac fermions of graphene all come from here.","level":"proved","demos":["band"]},{"id":"fermi","n":"04","title":"Fermi liquids: why strongly interacting electrons still look free","summary":"In a metal the Coulomb energy between electrons is about as large as their kinetic energy, so in principle they cannot be treated as independent. Landau’s Fermi-liquid theory resolves the paradox: switch the interaction on slowly and the excitations near the Fermi surface turn one by one into quasiparticles, with a changed mass and a small residual interaction, living longer the closer they are to the Fermi surface. In heavy-fermion compounds quasiparticles can be hundreds of times heavier than electrons yet still form a Fermi liquid; strange metals may not.","level":"approx","demos":["fermi"]},{"id":"magnons","n":"05","title":"Magnons: quanta of spin waves","summary":"In a magnetically ordered ground state every spin is neatly aligned. The lowest excitation is not flipping one spin but sharing the flip among all the spins as a wave: a spin wave. Its quantum, the magnon, is a boson carrying one unit of spin angular momentum. Bloch used it to explain the T^{3/2} fall of magnetisation at low temperature; neutron scattering measures its dispersion directly; today magnons carry information and have even been condensed at room temperature.","level":"proved","demos":["magnon"]},{"id":"excitons","n":"06","title":"Excitons: a “hydrogen atom” of an electron and a hole","summary":"Light in a semiconductor or insulator lifts out an electron and leaves a hole. The two attract and can bind into a neutral quasiparticle that roams the crystal: the exciton. It has its own Rydberg series and comes in small (Frenkel) and large (Wannier) varieties; Rydberg excitons about 2 micrometres across have been seen in Cu₂O, and in monolayer two-dimensional materials excitons are bound so tightly that they survive at room temperature. Photovoltaics, light emission and energy transfer in photosynthesis all pass through them.","level":"tested","demos":["exciton"]},{"id":"polarons","n":"07","title":"Polarons: an electron dressed in the lattice","summary":"In ionic crystals and polar semiconductors an electron pushes and pulls the surrounding ions and gets trapped in the polarisation it creates. The electron plus this distortion is a polaron: heavier than the bare electron, lower in energy, and with a different mobility. At weak coupling it is a nearly free “large polaron”; at strong coupling the electron may be trapped on one site, a “small polaron” that moves by thermally activated hopping. Feynman’s 1955 path-integral solution remains the benchmark for the problem.","level":"approx","demos":["polaron"]},{"id":"plasmons","n":"08","title":"Plasmons: the electron sea oscillating as a whole","summary":"Push a metal’s electron sea as a whole and the positive ionic background pulls it back, so the whole sea oscillates at the plasma frequency; its quantum is the plasmon, about 15 eV in aluminium. It explains why metals shine, why alkali metals are transparent in the ultraviolet, and why fast electrons crossing a thin film lose energy in portions. Plasmons on surfaces and nanoparticles squeeze light to scales far below the wavelength and star in nanophotonics and sensing.","level":"tested","demos":["plasmon"]},{"id":"polaritons","n":"09","title":"Polaritons: half light, half matter","summary":"When light inside a material matches the frequency of some material excitation (an optical phonon, an exciton, a plasmon) and couples strongly enough, “photon” and “excitation” can no longer be told apart: they mix into two new modes that repel where they would have crossed. Such a mixed quasiparticle is a polariton. Huang Kun wrote its equations for light and optical phonons in 1951; today exciton-polaritons in semiconductor microcavities weigh around a ten-thousandth of an electron and condense at a few kelvin or even room temperature, and polaritons in two-dimensional materials squeeze infrared light into a few tens of nanometres.","level":"tested","demos":["polariton"]},{"id":"cooper","n":"10","title":"Cooper pairs and Bogoliubov quasiparticles: excitations of a superconductor","summary":"If two electrons near the Fermi surface feel even a slight net attraction, they bind into a Cooper pair; when all the pairs condense into one quantum state, the result is superconductivity. A single-electron excitation must then break a pair, costing an energy gap 2Δ, and what gets excited is a Bogoliubov quasiparticle: part electron and part hole, without a definite charge. BCS theory predicted the gap size 2Δ ≈ 3.53 k_BT_c and its temperature dependence, and Giaever’s tunnelling experiments saw it directly.","level":"tested","demos":["bcs"]},{"id":"anyons","n":"11","title":"Anyons and Majoranas: quasiparticles that exist only in two dimensions","summary":"Exchange two identical particles in three-dimensional space and the wavefunction can only be multiplied by +1 (bosons) or −1 (fermions). In two dimensions an exchange can bring any phase, and such quasiparticles are called anyons. Quasiparticles of the fractional quantum Hall effect carry a third of the electron charge; noise measurements “counted” this charge, and two experiments in 2020 saw their fractional statistics. Stranger “non-Abelian” anyons change the state of the system when exchanged and could serve noise-resistant topological quantum computing; Majorana zero modes at the ends of topological superconductors are candidates, though the experimental evidence remains disputed.","level":"frontier","demos":["kitaev"]},{"id":"measure","n":"12","title":"How quasiparticles are seen","summary":"Quasiparticles cannot be seen or touched, yet every kind can be measured: send in a probe (neutron, photon, electron), measure how much energy and momentum it lost, and you get the quasiparticle’s dispersion and lifetime. Neutrons see phonons and magnons; light sees optical phonons, excitons and polaritons; electron energy loss sees plasmons; tunnelling sees gaps; noise sees charge; and angle-resolved photoemission gives the electron spectral function directly, down to the “kink” left by phonons.","level":"tested","demos":["arpes"]}],"milestones":[{"year":"1879","text":"The Hall effect: a current in a magnetic field produces a transverse voltage whose sign gives the carrier charge (<a href=\"#c-holes\" data-open=\"holes\">03 · Holes and effective mass: band electrons are already quasiparticles</a>) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hall1879\" data-ref=\"hall1879\">Hall 1879</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1907","text":"Einstein applies quanta to vibrations in solids: the starting point of phonons <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-einstein1907\" data-ref=\"einstein1907\">Einstein 1907</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1930","text":"Bloch proposes spin waves and predicts that magnetisation falls as T^{3/2} <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bloch1930\" data-ref=\"bloch1930\">Bloch 1930</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1931","text":"Frenkel’s “excitation waves”: the exciton concept <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-frenkel1931\" data-ref=\"frenkel1931\">Frenkel 1931</a>]</span>; Wannier’s large-radius exciton follows in 1937 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wannier1937\" data-ref=\"wannier1937\">Wannier 1937</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1941","text":"Landau explains superfluid helium with two kinds of elementary excitation, phonons and rotons <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-landau1941\" data-ref=\"landau1941\">Landau 1941</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1951","text":"Huang Kun’s phonon-polariton equations <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-huang1951\" data-ref=\"huang1951\">Huang 1951</a>]</span>; Bohm and Pines write plasmons as collective coordinates <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bohm1951\" data-ref=\"bohm1951\">Bohm &amp; Pines 1951</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1954","text":"Fröhlich’s large-polaron model <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-froehlich1954\" data-ref=\"froehlich1954\">Fröhlich 1954</a>]</span>; Feynman solves it with path integrals the next year <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-feynman1955\" data-ref=\"feynman1955\">Feynman 1955</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1957","text":"BCS theory <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bcs1957\" data-ref=\"bcs1957\">Bardeen, Cooper &amp; Schrieffer 1957</a>]</span>; Ritchie predicts surface plasmons <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ritchie1957\" data-ref=\"ritchie1957\">Ritchie 1957</a>]</span>; neutrons detect magnons for the first time <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-brockhouse1957\" data-ref=\"brockhouse1957\">Brockhouse 1957</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1960","text":"Giaever measures the superconducting gap directly with tunnel junctions <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-giaever1960\" data-ref=\"giaever1960\">Giaever 1960</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1982","text":"The fractional quantum Hall effect <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-tsui1982\" data-ref=\"tsui1982\">Tsui, Stormer &amp; Gossard 1982</a>]</span>; Wilczek names anyons <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wilczek1982\" data-ref=\"wilczek1982\">Wilczek 1982</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1992","text":"Strong coupling in a semiconductor microcavity: the Rabi splitting of exciton-polaritons <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-weisbuch1992\" data-ref=\"weisbuch1992\">Weisbuch et al. 1992</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1997","text":"Shot noise measures fractional charge e/3 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-depicciotto1997\" data-ref=\"depicciotto1997\">de-Picciotto et al. 1997</a>; <a href=\"#ref-saminadayar1997\" data-ref=\"saminadayar1997\">Saminadayar et al. 1997</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2006","text":"Bose–Einstein condensation of exciton-polaritons <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kasprzak2006\" data-ref=\"kasprzak2006\">Kasprzak et al. 2006</a>]</span>; condensation of pumped magnons at room temperature <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-demokritov2006\" data-ref=\"demokritov2006\">Demokritov et al. 2006</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2012","text":"Zero-bias peaks in nanowire devices: the first experimental signals of Majorana zero modes <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-mourik2012\" data-ref=\"mourik2012\">Mourik et al. 2012</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2014","text":"Giant Rydberg excitons up to n = 25 in Cu₂O <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kazimierczuk2014\" data-ref=\"kazimierczuk2014\">Kazimierczuk et al. 2014</a>]</span>; the non-hydrogenic exciton series of monolayer WS₂ <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-chernikov2014\" data-ref=\"chernikov2014\">Chernikov et al. 2014</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2020","text":"Two experiments see the fractional statistics of anyons <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bartolomei2020\" data-ref=\"bartolomei2020\">Bartolomei et al. 2020</a>; <a href=\"#ref-nakamura2020\" data-ref=\"nakamura2020\">Nakamura et al. 2020</a>]</span>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the most direct test of whether an excitation is a good quasiparticle?","opts":["Whether it is an elementary particle","The spectral function has a peak much narrower than its energy","Whether it can leave the material","Whether it is a boson"],"a":1,"why":"The width Γ is the decay rate; only with Γ ≪ |E| does it oscillate many periods before decaying.","ch":"what"},{"q":"Why is the total number of phonons not conserved?","opts":["Because phonons leave the crystal","Because they are excitation numbers of oscillators, changing whenever the energy does (zero chemical potential)","Because phonons are fermions","It actually is conserved"],"a":1,"why":"Phonons appear from nowhere as the temperature rises; the equilibrium distribution is Bose–Einstein with μ = 0.","ch":"phonons"},{"q":"Why is the effective mass of a hole in an almost full band positive?","opts":["Because holes are positively charged","Because electrons at the band top have negative effective mass, and the hole’s mass is its opposite","Because holes are heavier than electrons","Because full bands carry no current"],"a":1,"why":"The band top has negative curvature, so electrons there have m* &lt; 0; treating the missing electron as a particle flips both mass and charge.","ch":"holes"},{"q":"How does the quasiparticle decay rate in a Fermi liquid vary with energy ε?","opts":["∝ ε","∝ ε²","Independent of ε","∝ 1/ε"],"a":1,"why":"The Pauli principle restricts phase space to ∝ ε², so quasiparticles improve towards the Fermi surface.","ch":"fermi"},{"q":"What are the long-wave magnon dispersions of a ferromagnet and an antiferromagnet?","opts":["Both ∝ k","Both ∝ k²","Ferro ∝ k², antiferro ∝ |k|","Ferro ∝ |k|, antiferro ∝ k²"],"a":2,"why":"A ferromagnet’s order parameter is conserved, giving a massive-particle dispersion; an antiferromagnet’s is phonon-like.","ch":"magnons"},{"q":"Why is the exciton binding energy of monolayer WS₂ much larger than in bulk?","opts":["Electrons are heavier in a monolayer","Two-dimensional geometry plus weaker screening: field lines run outside into low-dielectric surroundings","The monolayer is a metal","Phonons bind them"],"a":1,"why":"Ideal 2D binding is already 4 times the 3D value, and weaker screening increases it much further.","ch":"excitons"},{"q":"What sets the strength of a Fröhlich polaron?","opts":["The electron’s charge","A single dimensionless coupling constant α","Temperature","The lattice constant"],"a":1,"why":"α depends on effective mass, the LO phonon frequency and 1/ε∞ − 1/ε₀: about 0.07 for GaAs, 3–5 for alkali halides.","ch":"polarons"},{"q":"What happens to light below the plasma frequency hitting a metal?","opts":["It is transmitted","It is almost totally reflected","It turns into phonons","Its frequency doubles"],"a":1,"why":"For ω &lt; ω_p/√ε∞, ε &lt; 0 and light cannot enter: metallic lustre.","ch":"plasmons"},{"q":"Why can microcavity exciton-polaritons condense at a few kelvin or even room temperature?","opts":["They are fermions","They are extremely light (about 10⁻⁴ electron masses), so their thermal wavelength is long","They do not interact with anything","There is no temperature in the cavity"],"a":1,"why":"The thermal de Broglie wavelength ∝ 1/√(mT); ten thousand times less mass allows degeneracy at much higher temperature.","ch":"polaritons"},{"q":"What is a Bogoliubov quasiparticle at the Fermi surface?","opts":["An electron","A hole","Half electron and half hole, with zero expected charge","A Cooper pair"],"a":2,"why":"At ξ = 0, u² = v² = ½; its energy is Δ and cannot be lower.","ch":"cooper"},{"q":"Why can anyons exist only in two dimensions?","opts":["Because there is no spin in 2D","Because in 2D a loop round another particle cannot be shrunk to a point","Because particles are massless in 2D","Because magnetic fields can only be 2D"],"a":1,"why":"In 3D two exchanges can be deformed into doing nothing, so the phase must be ±1; not in 2D.","ch":"anyons"},{"q":"What can a kink in an ARPES band tell you?","opts":["The sample temperature","The energy of a bosonic mode coupled to the electrons, and the coupling constant λ","The wavelength of the light source","The sample thickness"],"a":1,"why":"The kink sits at ħω_E, and the velocity near the Fermi level is divided by 1 + λ.","ch":"measure"}],"books":[{"title":"Solid State Physics","authors":"Neil W. Ashcroft, N. David Mermin (Saunders, 1976)","note":"The standard textbook on bands, holes, effective mass, phonons, magnons and superconductivity, with complete derivations."},{"title":"Introduction to Solid State Physics","authors":"Charles Kittel (Wiley, 8th ed. 2005)","note":"An entry point for almost every chapter; chapter 14 covers plasmons, polaritons and polarons, chapter 15 excitons."},{"title":"Quantum Theory of the Electron Liquid","authors":"Gabriele F. Giuliani, Giovanni Vignale (Cambridge, 2005)","note":"A systematic treatment of Fermi liquids, screening and plasmons, for after chapters 4 and 8."},{"title":"Condensed Matter Field Theory","authors":"Alexander Altland, Ben Simons (Cambridge, 2nd ed. 2010)","note":"A unified field-theory treatment of quasiparticles, self-energies and spectral functions; the mathematics of chapter 1 is here."},{"title":"Introduction to Superconductivity","authors":"Michael Tinkham (McGraw-Hill, 2nd ed. 1996)","note":"The classic textbook on BCS theory, Bogoliubov quasiparticles and tunnelling."},{"title":"Microcavities","authors":"Alexey V. Kavokin, Jeremy J. Baumberg, Guillaume Malpuech, Fabrice P. Laussy (Oxford, 2nd ed. 2017)","note":"Exciton-polaritons and their condensation, from strong coupling to experimental techniques."},{"title":"Quantum Field Theory of Many-Body Systems","authors":"Xiao-Gang Wen (Oxford, 2004)","note":"An introduction to topological order, fractional quantum Hall states and anyons: background for chapter 11."}],"open":[{"q":"How should metals without quasiparticles (strange metals) be described? Where does their linear resistivity come from?","ch":"fermi"},{"q":"What is the pairing mechanism of the high-temperature cuprate superconductors? Are the ARPES kinks mainly from phonons or from magnetic excitations?","ch":"measure"},{"q":"Do non-Abelian anyons exist? Is the ν = 5/2 state really the Moore–Read state?","ch":"anyons"},{"q":"Are the zero-bias peaks in nanowires really Majorana zero modes? Can the first braiding experiment be done?","ch":"anyons"},{"q":"Can polariton condensates be turned into low-threshold lasers, simulators or optical logic devices?","ch":"polaritons"},{"q":"Can one reliably predict from first principles whether electrons in a material form large polarons, small polarons or free carriers?","ch":"polarons"}],"notes":{"map":"Each card is a chapter, grouped by kind of quasiparticle; chapter numbers give the suggested reading order. The label gives the status of the bulk of the chapter; key points inside carry their own labels. Chapters you have read are marked (on this device only).","history":"Years are publication years.","path":"Read chapter 1 first, then jump as you like. To go deep on phonons, visit the <a href=\"../quasiparticles/phonons/\">Phonon hall</a>; to see what quasiparticles look like in spectra, the <a href=\"../spectra/\">Spectra hall</a>; for the optics behind excitons and polaritons, <a href=\"../optics/\">Optics</a>; for Majoranas and qubits, <a href=\"../quantum/computing/\">Quantum computers</a>; for the statistical-mechanics groundwork (Bose–Einstein and Fermi–Dirac distributions), <a href=\"../macro/\">Heat, Statistics and Flow</a>.","library":"57 references. Each was looked up by DOI in OpenAlex (data from Crossref) for title, authors, year and volume (November 2026; node tools/quasi-refs.mjs rechecks them)."}},"refs":[{"id":"hall1879","authors":"E. H. Hall","year":1879,"title":"On a new action of the magnet on electric currents","journal":"American Journal of Mathematics","vol":"2","pages":"287–292","doi":"10.2307/2369245","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["holes"]},{"id":"einstein1907","authors":"A. Einstein","year":1907,"title":"Die Plancksche Theorie der Strahlung und die Theorie der spezifischen Wärme","journal":"Annalen der Physik","vol":"327","pages":"180–190","doi":"10.1002/andp.19063270110","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["phonons"]},{"id":"debye1912","authors":"P. Debye","year":1912,"title":"Zur Theorie der spezifischen Wärmen","journal":"Annalen der Physik","vol":"344","pages":"789–839","doi":"10.1002/andp.19123441404","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["phonons"]},{"id":"bloch1930","authors":"F. Bloch","year":1930,"title":"Zur Theorie des Ferromagnetismus","journal":"Zeitschrift für Physik","vol":"61","pages":"206–219","doi":"10.1007/BF01339661","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["magnons"]},{"id":"frenkel1931","authors":"J. Frenkel","year":1931,"title":"On the transformation of light into heat in solids. I","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"37","pages":"17–44","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.37.17","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["excitons"]},{"id":"wannier1937","authors":"G. H. Wannier","year":1937,"title":"The structure of electronic excitation levels in insulating crystals","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"52","pages":"191–197","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.52.191","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["excitons"]},{"id":"holstein1940","authors":"T. Holstein, H. Primakoff","year":1940,"title":"Field dependence of the intrinsic domain magnetization of a ferromagnet","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"58","pages":"1098–1113","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.58.1098","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["magnons"]},{"id":"landau1941","authors":"L. Landau","year":1941,"title":"Theory of the superfluidity of helium II","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"60","pages":"356–358","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.60.356","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["what"]},{"id":"bohm1951","authors":"D. Bohm, D. Pines","year":1951,"title":"A collective description of electron interactions. I. Magnetic interactions","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"82","pages":"625–634","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.82.625","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["plasmons"]},{"id":"haynes1951","authors":"J. R. Haynes, W. Shockley","year":1951,"title":"The mobility and life of injected holes and electrons in germanium","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"81","pages":"835–843","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.81.835","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["holes"]},{"id":"huang1951","authors":"K. Huang","year":1951,"title":"On the interaction between the radiation field and ionic crystals","journal":"Proceedings of the Royal Society A","vol":"208","pages":"352–365","doi":"10.1098/rspa.1951.0166","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["polaritons"]},{"id":"pines1952","authors":"D. Pines, D. Bohm","year":1952,"title":"A collective description of electron interactions: II. Collective vs individual particle aspects of the interactions","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"85","pages":"338–353","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.85.338","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["plasmons"]},{"id":"froehlich1954","authors":"H. Fröhlich","year":1954,"title":"Electrons in lattice fields","journal":"Advances in Physics","vol":"3","pages":"325–361","doi":"10.1080/00018735400101213","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["polarons"]},{"id":"brockhouse1955","authors":"B. N. Brockhouse, A. T. Stewart","year":1955,"title":"Scattering of neutrons by phonons in an aluminum single crystal","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"100","pages":"756–757","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.100.756","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["phonons","measure"]},{"id":"dresselhaus1955","authors":"G. Dresselhaus, A. F. Kip, C. Kittel","year":1955,"title":"Cyclotron resonance of electrons and holes in silicon and germanium crystals","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"98","pages":"368–384","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.98.368","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["holes","measure"]},{"id":"feynman1955","authors":"R. P. Feynman","year":1955,"title":"Slow electrons in a polar crystal","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"97","pages":"660–665","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.97.660","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["polarons"]},{"id":"cooper1956","authors":"L. N. Cooper","year":1956,"title":"Bound electron pairs in a degenerate Fermi gas","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"104","pages":"1189–1190","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.104.1189","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["cooper"]},{"id":"bcs1957","authors":"J. Bardeen, L. N. Cooper, J. R. Schrieffer","year":1957,"title":"Theory of superconductivity","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"108","pages":"1175–1204","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.108.1175","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["cooper"]},{"id":"brockhouse1957","authors":"B. N. Brockhouse","year":1957,"title":"Scattering of neutrons by spin waves in magnetite","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"106","pages":"859–864","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.106.859","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["magnons","measure"]},{"id":"ritchie1957","authors":"R. H. Ritchie","year":1957,"title":"Plasma losses by fast electrons in thin films","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"106","pages":"874–881","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.106.874","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["plasmons"]},{"id":"bogoliubov1958","authors":"N. N. Bogoliubov","year":1958,"title":"On a new method in the theory of superconductivity","journal":"Il Nuovo Cimento","vol":"7","pages":"794–805","doi":"10.1007/BF02745585","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["cooper"]},{"id":"hopfield1958","authors":"J. J. Hopfield","year":1958,"title":"Theory of the contribution of excitons to the complex dielectric constant of crystals","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"112","pages":"1555–1567","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.112.1555","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["polaritons"]},{"id":"powell1959","authors":"C. J. Powell, J. B. Swan","year":1959,"title":"Origin of the characteristic electron energy losses in aluminum","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"115","pages":"869–875","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.115.869","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["plasmons","measure"]},{"id":"giaever1960","authors":"I. Giaever","year":1960,"title":"Energy gap in superconductors measured by electron tunneling","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"5","pages":"147–148","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.5.147","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["cooper","measure"]},{"id":"henry1965","authors":"C. H. Henry, J. J. Hopfield","year":1965,"title":"Raman scattering by polaritons","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"15","pages":"964–966","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.15.964","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["polaritons","measure"]},{"id":"anderson1972","authors":"P. W. Anderson","year":1972,"title":"More is different","journal":"Science","vol":"177","pages":"393–396","doi":"10.1126/science.177.4047.393","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["what"]},{"id":"leinaas1977","authors":"J. M. Leinaas, J. Myrheim","year":1977,"title":"On the theory of identical particles","journal":"Il Nuovo Cimento B","vol":"37","pages":"1–23","doi":"10.1007/BF02727953","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anyons"]},{"id":"tsui1982","authors":"D. C. Tsui, H. L. Stormer, A. C. Gossard","year":1982,"title":"Two-dimensional magnetotransport in the extreme quantum limit","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"48","pages":"1559–1562","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.48.1559","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anyons"]},{"id":"wilczek1982","authors":"F. Wilczek","year":1982,"title":"Quantum mechanics of fractional-spin particles","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"49","pages":"957–959","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.49.957","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anyons"]},{"id":"laughlin1983","authors":"R. B. Laughlin","year":1983,"title":"Anomalous quantum Hall effect: an incompressible quantum fluid with fractionally charged excitations","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"50","pages":"1395–1398","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.50.1395","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anyons"]},{"id":"stewart1984","authors":"G. R. Stewart","year":1984,"title":"Heavy-fermion systems","journal":"Reviews of Modern Physics","vol":"56","pages":"755–787","doi":"10.1103/RevModPhys.56.755","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["fermi"]},{"id":"kadowaki1986","authors":"K. Kadowaki, S. B. Woods","year":1986,"title":"Universal relationship of the resistivity and specific heat in heavy-fermion compounds","journal":"Solid State Communications","vol":"58","pages":"507–509","doi":"10.1016/0038-1098(86)90785-4","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["fermi"]},{"id":"weisbuch1992","authors":"C. Weisbuch, M. Nishioka, A. Ishikawa, Y. Arakawa","year":1992,"title":"Observation of the coupled exciton-photon mode splitting in a semiconductor quantum microcavity","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"69","pages":"3314–3317","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.69.3314","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["polaritons"]},{"id":"depicciotto1997","authors":"R. de-Picciotto, M. Reznikov, M. Heiblum, V. Umansky, G. Bunin, D. Mahalu","year":1997,"title":"Direct observation of a fractional charge","journal":"Nature","vol":"389","pages":"162–164","doi":"10.1038/38241","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anyons"]},{"id":"saminadayar1997","authors":"L. Saminadayar, D. C. Glattli, Y. Jin, B. Etienne","year":1997,"title":"Observation of the e/3 fractionally charged Laughlin quasiparticle","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"79","pages":"2526–2529","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.79.2526","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anyons"]},{"id":"kitaev2001","authors":"A. Yu. Kitaev","year":2001,"title":"Unpaired Majorana fermions in quantum wires","journal":"Physics-Uspekhi","vol":"44","pages":"131–136","doi":"10.1070/1063-7869/44/10S/S29","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anyons"]},{"id":"lanzara2001","authors":"A. Lanzara, P. V. Bogdanov, X. J. Zhou, S. A. Kellar, D. L. Feng, E. D. Lu, T. Yoshida, H. Eisaki, A. Fujimori, K. Kishio, J.-I. Shimoyama, T. Noda, S. Uchida, Z. Hussain, Z.-X. Shen","year":2001,"title":"Evidence for ubiquitous strong electron–phonon coupling in high-temperature superconductors","journal":"Nature","vol":"412","pages":"510–514","doi":"10.1038/35087518","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["measure"]},{"id":"stewart2001","authors":"G. R. Stewart","year":2001,"title":"Non-Fermi-liquid behavior in d- and f-electron metals","journal":"Reviews of Modern Physics","vol":"73","pages":"797–855","doi":"10.1103/RevModPhys.73.797","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["fermi"]},{"id":"barnes2003","authors":"W. L. Barnes, A. Dereux, T. W. Ebbesen","year":2003,"title":"Surface plasmon subwavelength optics","journal":"Nature","vol":"424","pages":"824–830","doi":"10.1038/nature01937","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["plasmons"]},{"id":"damascelli2003","authors":"A. Damascelli, Z. Hussain, Z.-X. Shen","year":2003,"title":"Angle-resolved photoemission studies of the cuprate superconductors","journal":"Reviews of Modern Physics","vol":"75","pages":"473–541","doi":"10.1103/RevModPhys.75.473","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["measure"]},{"id":"kitaev2003","authors":"A. Yu. Kitaev","year":2003,"title":"Fault-tolerant quantum computation by anyons","journal":"Annals of Physics","vol":"303","pages":"2–30","doi":"10.1016/S0003-4916(02)00018-0","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anyons"]},{"id":"novoselov2005","authors":"K. S. Novoselov, A. K. Geim, S. V. Morozov, D. Jiang, M. I. Katsnelson, I. V. Grigorieva, S. V. Dubonos, A. A. Firsov","year":2005,"title":"Two-dimensional gas of massless Dirac fermions in graphene","journal":"Nature","vol":"438","pages":"197–200","doi":"10.1038/nature04233","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["holes"]},{"id":"demokritov2006","authors":"S. O. Demokritov, V. E. Demidov, O. Dzyapko, G. A. Melkov, A. A. Serga, B. Hillebrands, A. N. Slavin","year":2006,"title":"Bose–Einstein condensation of quasi-equilibrium magnons at room temperature under pumping","journal":"Nature","vol":"443","pages":"430–433","doi":"10.1038/nature05117","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["magnons"]},{"id":"kasprzak2006","authors":"J. Kasprzak, M. Richard, S. Kundermann, A. Baas, P. Jeambrun, J. M. J. Keeling, F. M. Marchetti, M. H. Szymańska, R. André, J. L. Staehli, V. Savona, P. B. Littlewood, B. Deveaud, Le Si Dang","year":2006,"title":"Bose–Einstein condensation of exciton polaritons","journal":"Nature","vol":"443","pages":"409–414","doi":"10.1038/nature05131","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["polaritons"]},{"id":"nayak2008","authors":"C. Nayak, S. H. Simon, A. Stern, M. Freedman, S. Das Sarma","year":2008,"title":"Non-Abelian anyons and topological quantum computation","journal":"Reviews of Modern Physics","vol":"80","pages":"1083–1159","doi":"10.1103/RevModPhys.80.1083","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anyons"]},{"id":"mak2010","authors":"K. F. Mak, C. Lee, J. Hone, J. Shan, T. F. Heinz","year":2010,"title":"Atomically thin MoS2: a new direct-gap semiconductor","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"105","pages":"136805","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.105.136805","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["excitons"]},{"id":"mourik2012","authors":"V. Mourik, K. Zuo, S. M. Frolov, S. R. Plissard, E. P. A. M. Bakkers, L. P. Kouwenhoven","year":2012,"title":"Signatures of Majorana fermions in hybrid superconductor-semiconductor nanowire devices","journal":"Science","vol":"336","pages":"1003–1007","doi":"10.1126/science.1222360","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anyons"]},{"id":"chernikov2014","authors":"A. Chernikov, T. C. Berkelbach, H. M. Hill, A. Rigosi, Y. Li, B. Aslan, D. R. Reichman, M. S. Hybertsen, T. F. Heinz","year":2014,"title":"Exciton binding energy and nonhydrogenic Rydberg series in monolayer WS2","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"113","pages":"076802","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.113.076802","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["excitons"]},{"id":"kazimierczuk2014","authors":"T. Kazimierczuk, D. Fröhlich, S. Scheel, H. Stolz, M. Bayer","year":2014,"title":"Giant Rydberg excitons in the copper oxide Cu2O","journal":"Nature","vol":"514","pages":"343–347","doi":"10.1038/nature13832","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["excitons"]},{"id":"chumak2015","authors":"A. V. Chumak, V. I. Vasyuchka, A. A. Serga, B. Hillebrands","year":2015,"title":"Magnon spintronics","journal":"Nature Physics","vol":"11","pages":"453–461","doi":"10.1038/nphys3347","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["magnons"]},{"id":"basov2016","authors":"D. N. Basov, M. M. Fogler, F. J. García de Abajo","year":2016,"title":"Polaritons in van der Waals materials","journal":"Science","vol":"354","pages":"aag1992","doi":"10.1126/science.aag1992","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["polaritons"]},{"id":"wang2018","authors":"G. Wang, A. Chernikov, M. M. Glazov, T. F. Heinz, X. Marie, T. Amand, B. Urbaszek","year":2018,"title":"Colloquium: Excitons in atomically thin transition metal dichalcogenides","journal":"Reviews of Modern Physics","vol":"90","pages":"021001","doi":"10.1103/RevModPhys.90.021001","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["excitons"]},{"id":"bartolomei2020","authors":"H. Bartolomei, M. Kumar, R. Bisognin, A. Marguerite, J.-M. Berroir, E. Bocquillon, B. Plaçais, A. Cavanna, Q. Dong, U. Gennser, Y. Jin, G. Fève","year":2020,"title":"Fractional statistics in anyon collisions","journal":"Science","vol":"368","pages":"173–177","doi":"10.1126/science.aaz5601","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anyons"]},{"id":"nakamura2020","authors":"J. Nakamura, S. Liang, G. C. Gardner, M. J. Manfra","year":2020,"title":"Direct observation of anyonic braiding statistics","journal":"Nature Physics","vol":"16","pages":"931–936","doi":"10.1038/s41567-020-1019-1","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anyons"]},{"id":"franchini2021","authors":"C. Franchini, M. Reticcioli, M. Setvin, U. Diebold","year":2021,"title":"Polarons in materials","journal":"Nature Reviews Materials","vol":"6","pages":"560–586","doi":"10.1038/s41578-021-00289-w","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["polarons"]},{"id":"sobota2021","authors":"J. A. Sobota, Y. He, Z.-X. Shen","year":2021,"title":"Angle-resolved photoemission studies of quantum materials","journal":"Reviews of Modern Physics","vol":"93","pages":"025006","doi":"10.1103/RevModPhys.93.025006","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["measure"]},{"id":"aghaee2023","authors":"M. Aghaee et al. (Microsoft Quantum)","year":2023,"title":"InAs-Al hybrid devices passing the topological gap protocol","journal":"Physical Review B","vol":"107","pages":"245423","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevB.107.245423","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["anyons"]}],"chapters":{"what":{"id":"what","body":[{"p":"When physicists describe a piece of copper, a crystal of silicon or a glass of superfluid helium, they almost never solve the Schrödinger equation for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>23</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> particles. They ask instead: push the system gently away from its ground state, and what gets excited? The answer is often surprisingly simple: something like a gas of particles flying through empty space, except that their “mass” and “charge” differ from those of particles in vacuum and they exist only inside the material. Landau applied this idea to superfluid helium, reducing its low-temperature properties to a gas of two kinds of “elementary excitations”, phonons and rotons <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-landau1941\" data-ref=\"landau1941\">Landau 1941</a>]</span>; applying the same idea to electrons in metals, spins in magnets and electron–hole pairs in semiconductors gave today’s whole family of “quasiparticles”."},{"p":"Anderson’s 1972 essay “More is different” turned this into a principle: every level of complexity has its own “elementary particles” and laws, which cannot be simply deduced from the level below yet are just as real <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-anderson1972\" data-ref=\"anderson1972\">Anderson 1972</a>]</span>. Quasiparticles are the best example of such “emergence”."},{"h":"Two kinds of quasiparticle"},{"list":["<b>Dressed particles</b>: a real particle (an electron, a hole) moves through the material dragging a cloud of disturbance (lattice distortion, screening charge, spin fluctuations). Its charge and spin are unchanged; its mass and lifetime are not. Examples: band electrons and holes (<a href=\"#c-holes\" data-open=\"holes\">03 · Holes and effective mass: band electrons are already quasiparticles</a>), Fermi-liquid quasiparticles (<a href=\"#c-fermi\" data-open=\"fermi\">04 · Fermi liquids: why strongly interacting electrons still look free</a>), polarons (<a href=\"#c-polarons\" data-open=\"polarons\">07 · Polarons: an electron dressed in the lattice</a>).","<b>Collective excitations</b>: a way for many particles to move in concert, appearing in discrete portions once quantised. Examples: phonons, where atoms vibrate together (<a href=\"#c-phonons\" data-open=\"phonons\">02 · Phonons: the first and cleanest quasiparticle</a>); magnons, where spins precess together (<a href=\"#c-magnons\" data-open=\"magnons\">05 · Magnons: quanta of spin waves</a>); plasmons, where the electron sea oscillates as a whole (<a href=\"#c-plasmons\" data-open=\"plasmons\">08 · Plasmons: the electron sea oscillating as a whole</a>). They are often bosons with no conserved number."]},{"p":"There is no sharp line between the two. An exciton (<a href=\"#c-excitons\" data-open=\"excitons\">06 · Excitons: a “hydrogen atom” of an electron and a hole</a>) is an electron and a hole bound together; a polariton (<a href=\"#c-polaritons\" data-open=\"polaritons\">09 · Polaritons: half light, half matter</a>) is a mixture of a photon and a material excitation; a Bogoliubov quasiparticle in a superconductor (<a href=\"#c-cooper\" data-open=\"cooper\">10 · Cooper pairs and Bogoliubov quasiparticles: excitations of a superconductor</a>) is part electron and part hole; the quasiparticles of the fractional quantum Hall effect (<a href=\"#c-anyons\" data-open=\"anyons\">11 · Anyons and Majoranas: quasiparticles that exist only in two dimensions</a>) carry a third of the electron’s charge and are neither bosons nor fermions."},{"h":"The criterion: a sharp peak in the spectral function"},{"p":"“Behaves like a particle” can be made precise. Add an electron of momentum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></semantics></math> to the system and ask for its energy distribution: the result is the <b>spectral function</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. For a free particle it is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> function at the dispersion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. With interactions the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> function becomes a peak of finite width on top of a broad background:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mi>π</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mtext>incoh</mtext></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mo>∫</mo><mi>A</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"The peak position <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is the quasiparticle dispersion (which defines the effective mass), the width <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is the decay rate (lifetime <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>), and the area under the peak <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> is the <b>quasiparticle weight</b>: how much of the bare electron we added survives as this quasiparticle. The quasiparticle makes sense when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mo>≪</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: it must oscillate many periods before it decays. In the demo below, adjust <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and see when the “particle” description breaks down."},{"demo":"spectral"},{"p":"The spectral function is not just a theoretical object. Angle-resolved photoemission measures <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> below the Fermi level; neutron, X-ray and electron energy-loss spectroscopies measure the corresponding “dynamic structure factor”. <a href=\"#c-measure\" data-open=\"measure\">12 · How quasiparticles are seen</a> is devoted to these measurements."},{"h":"Why low-energy excitations are often good quasiparticles"},{"p":"For an excitation to decay it must find an “exit” that conserves energy and momentum, splitting itself into other excitations. The lower the energy, the fewer final states are available. In a Fermi liquid the decay rate of quasiparticles shrinks as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> (<a href=\"#c-fermi\" data-open=\"fermi\">04 · Fermi liquids: why strongly interacting electrons still look free</a>); acoustic phonons and ferromagnetic magnons also become harder to decay at long wavelength. So at low enough temperature and energy most materials can be described as a dilute gas of quasiparticles, and the heat capacity, resistivity and susceptibility are all properties of that gas. Conversely, at higher energy or temperature, or with too strong interactions, the peak broadens and sinks into the background; in materials such as “strange metals” there may be no good quasiparticles at all."},{"p":"Different quasiparticles live on different energy scales, which decides what can measure them and at what temperature they can still be seen:"},{"demo":"zoo"},{"note":"<b>Are quasiparticles “real”?</b> They cannot be taken out of the material, but inside it they are measurable: they have a definite dispersion, charge, spin and statistics, and can be scattered, created and annihilated. Fractionally charged quasiparticles have even been “counted” in noise measurements (<a href=\"#c-anyons\" data-open=\"anyons\">11 · Anyons and Majoranas: quasiparticles that exist only in two dimensions</a>). Calling quasiparticles “only an approximation” is fair, but in the same sense the electron of particle physics is an excitation of a quantum field.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"Elementary excitations","p":"Low-energy excitations can often be written as a gas of nearly independent quasiparticles; Landau described superfluid helium with phonons and rotons.","st":"history"},{"h":"Two kinds","p":"Dressed particles (electrons, holes, polarons) and collective excitations (phonons, magnons, plasmons).","st":"tested"},{"h":"The spectral-function criterion","p":"A(k, ω) has a peak of width Γ ≪ |E|; the area Z under it is the quasiparticle weight.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Better at low energy","p":"Phase space for decay shrinks with energy, so quasiparticles live longer at low energy and temperature.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Metals without quasiparticles","p":"In strongly correlated systems such as strange metals there may be no good quasiparticles, and how to describe them is an open problem.","st":"open"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>π</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>G</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Green’s function and self-energy: every interaction effect is hidden in Σ."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Quasiparticle energy and decay rate."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">∣</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">]</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The quasiparticle weight."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The quasiparticle part decays exponentially in time."}],"example":{"q":"ARPES finds a quasiparticle peak 40 meV below the Fermi level with a half-width at half-maximum of 8 meV. What is its lifetime? Roughly how many periods does it oscillate before decaying to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math>?","steps":["Lifetime <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6.58</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mtext>meV</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>8</mn><mtext> meV</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>8.2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> s, about 82 fs.","Oscillation period <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4.14</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mtext>meV</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>40</mn><mtext> meV</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>1.03</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> s. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>8</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.80</mn></mrow></semantics></math>."],"a":"About 82 fs: it decays in less than one period. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> the quasiparticle is already “marginal” at this energy. Closer to the Fermi level the peak narrows and the quasiparticle improves."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-landau1941\" data-ref=\"landau1941\">Landau 1941</a> · <a href=\"#ref-anderson1972\" data-ref=\"anderson1972\">Anderson 1972</a>"},"phonons":{"id":"phonons","body":[{"p":"Atoms in a crystal are tied to their lattice sites by chemical bonds. At small amplitude the potential energy is quadratic in the displacements and the vibration splits into uncoupled <b>normal modes</b>, each with a definite wavevector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. Each normal mode is a harmonic oscillator whose energy, once quantised, can only be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. Read <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></semantics></math> as “this mode holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></semantics></math> phonons”, and the vibrating crystal becomes a gas of phonons."},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"This is the cleanest quasiparticle: in the harmonic approximation it is <b>exactly</b> non-interacting with infinite lifetime, so the quasiparticle picture is a theorem rather than an approximation. In real crystals the potential has cubic and quartic terms, phonons collide and decay with finite lifetimes, and this is precisely the origin of thermal resistance and thermal expansion."},{"h":"Three hallmarks of a quasiparticle"},{"list":["<b>No conserved number.</b> Raise the temperature and phonons appear “from nowhere”; their chemical potential is zero. The demo below counts the mean number of phonons per atom.","<b>Crystal momentum.</b> A phonon carries <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is defined only up to a reciprocal-lattice vector and is not the atoms’ total mechanical momentum. It is conserved in collisions up to a reciprocal-lattice vector; “umklapp processes” are the source of thermal resistance.","<b>Exists only in the material.</b> Melt the crystal or remove the atoms and the phonon is gone. Its “particle-ness” is a way for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>23</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> atoms to move together."]},{"demo":"count"},{"h":"From Einstein to neutrons"},{"p":"Phonons have the oldest story among quasiparticles. In 1907 Einstein applied Planck’s quanta to the vibrations of a solid and explained for the first time why the heat capacity falls at low temperature <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-einstein1907\" data-ref=\"einstein1907\">Einstein 1907</a>]</span>; in 1912 Debye replaced the single frequency with continuum sound waves and obtained the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> law of low-temperature heat capacity <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-debye1912\" data-ref=\"debye1912\">Debye 1912</a>]</span>. The name “phonon” was coined in the 1930s on the model of “photon”. More than forty years later, Brockhouse measured the phonon dispersion of aluminium directly by inelastic neutron scattering <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-brockhouse1955\" data-ref=\"brockhouse1955\">Brockhouse &amp; Stewart 1955</a>]</span>: a neutron comes in, emits or absorbs one phonon, and its change in energy and momentum gives the phonon’s <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. From then on quasiparticles were not just a convenient theoretical language but something measurable mode by mode."},{"p":"Phonons are also the “clothing” of other quasiparticles: an electron dragging a lattice distortion becomes a polaron (<a href=\"#c-polarons\" data-open=\"polarons\">07 · Polarons: an electron dressed in the lattice</a>), exchanging phonons pairs electrons into Cooper pairs (<a href=\"#c-cooper\" data-open=\"cooper\">10 · Cooper pairs and Bogoliubov quasiparticles: excitations of a superconductor</a>), light and optical phonons mix into phonon-polaritons (<a href=\"#c-polaritons\" data-open=\"polaritons\">09 · Polaritons: half light, half matter</a>), and kinks in ARPES bands are often left by phonons (<a href=\"#c-measure\" data-open=\"measure\">12 · How quasiparticles are seen</a>)."},{"note":"<b>The details are in the <a href=\"../quasiparticles/phonons/\">Phonon hall</a>.</b> Nine chapters: the one-dimensional chain, the diatomic chain and optical branches, three-dimensional crystals and the Brillouin zone, quantisation and the density of states, heat capacity, how phonons are measured (neutrons, X-rays, Raman), anharmonicity and thermal expansion, thermal conductivity, and computing phonons from first principles and machine-learned potentials; eleven live demos and 53 DOI-checked references.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"One oscillator per normal mode","p":"The harmonic crystal’s Hamiltonian splits exactly into oscillators, and excitation numbers are phonon numbers.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Bosons with zero chemical potential","p":"In equilibrium ⟨n⟩ is the Bose–Einstein distribution with μ = 0; the phonon number changes with temperature.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Lifetimes come from anharmonicity","p":"Cubic and quartic terms make phonons collide and decay, giving thermal resistance and thermal expansion.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Measurable mode by mode","p":"Inelastic neutron scattering measures ω(q) directly; inelastic X-ray scattering and Raman followed.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mi>T</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mfrac><msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mtext>  </mtext><mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"3.0em\">→</mo><mpadded width=\"+0.6em\" lspace=\"0.3em\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></mpadded></mover><mtext>  </mtext><mn>18</mn><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mi>T</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mfrac><msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Phonons per atom in the Debye model."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>≡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Crystal momentum is defined only up to a reciprocal-lattice vector."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>∓</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Conservation laws when a neutron emits (upper sign) or absorbs (lower sign) one phonon."}],"example":{"q":"Copper has a Debye temperature of about 343 K and an atom density of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8.5</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>22</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> cm⁻³. Roughly how many thermal phonons are there per cubic centimetre at 10 K?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>343</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0292</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, deep in the low-temperature limit, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>18</mn><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>21.6</mn><mo>×</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.0292</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>5.4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>.","Multiply by the atom density: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>8.5</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>22</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4.6</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>19</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> cm⁻³."],"a":"About <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>19</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> per cubic centimetre, one for every two thousand atoms. By room temperature there are more than two phonons per atom on average (pick copper in the demo and slide to 300 K)."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-einstein1907\" data-ref=\"einstein1907\">Einstein 1907</a> · <a href=\"#ref-debye1912\" data-ref=\"debye1912\">Debye 1912</a> · <a href=\"#ref-brockhouse1955\" data-ref=\"brockhouse1955\">Brockhouse &amp; Stewart 1955</a>"},"holes":{"id":"holes","body":[{"p":"An electron in vacuum has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. In a crystal it feels the periodic potential of the nuclei and other electrons. Bloch’s theorem says the eigenstates can still be labelled by a crystal momentum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, but the energy becomes a set of bands <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> separated by gaps. This is already a quasiparticle: no longer “an electron in vacuum” but one wearing the clothing of the lattice."},{"h":"Effective mass"},{"p":"An external force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi></mrow></semantics></math> changes the crystal momentum as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">F</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, and the electron’s velocity is the slope of the band, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Together, the ratio of acceleration to force is set by the curvature of the band:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>E</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac><mtext>  </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>near a band edge</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"At the bottom of a band the curvature is positive and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; the wider and more “curved” the band, the lighter the electron. Electrons in the GaAs conduction band weigh only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.067</mn><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, which is why they are fast and highly mobile. At the top of a band the curvature is negative and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> is <b>negative</b>: a force that pushes it makes it go backwards. This does not violate Newton’s laws, because the external force is only part of the force on the electron; the rest comes from the lattice, and the effective mass absorbs it."},{"h":"Holes"},{"p":"A completely filled band carries no current: for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></semantics></math> there is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, the velocities cancel in pairs, and an electric field only shifts all the electrons together, so a full band stays full. So in an <b>almost</b> full band, conduction depends only on the few empty states. Treat “a missing electron” as a particle: its charge is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, its velocity equals that of the empty state, and its mass is minus the effective mass of the empty state, which at the top of the band is <b>positive</b>. This is a <b>hole</b>. The demo below sums the velocities of all the electrons, and separately sums only the empty states treated as positive charges, and gets exactly the same current."},{"demo":"band"},{"p":"The positive charge of holes can be measured. In 1879 Hall found that a current in a magnetic field produces a transverse voltage in a conductor <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hall1879\" data-ref=\"hall1879\">Hall 1879</a>]</span>; the sign of the voltage tells whether the carriers are positive or negative. Metals such as beryllium, zinc and cadmium were later found to have positive Hall coefficients, contradicting the picture of “current as flowing electrons”; holes in band theory explained it. In semiconductors holes matter even more: p-type semiconductors conduct mainly by holes. In 1951 Haynes and Shockley injected a pulse of holes into a germanium bar and watched it drift in an electric field, spread out and recombine with electrons, measuring the mobility and lifetime of holes directly <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-haynes1951\" data-ref=\"haynes1951\">Haynes &amp; Shockley 1951</a>]</span>."},{"p":"The effective mass can also be measured directly. Put a semiconductor in a magnetic field and the carriers circle in cyclotron orbits at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math>; measure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> by resonant microwave absorption and you get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math>. In 1955 Dresselhaus, Kip and Kittel measured the effective masses of electrons and holes in silicon and germanium by cyclotron resonance and found that holes come in “heavy” and “light” varieties (about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.3</mn><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.04</mn><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> in germanium), corresponding to two bands at the top of the valence band <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-dresselhaus1955\" data-ref=\"dresselhaus1955\">Dresselhaus, Kip &amp; Kittel 1955</a>]</span>."},{"h":"Massless quasiparticles"},{"p":"Bands need not be parabolas. Near the K point of graphene’s Brillouin zone the conduction and valence bands meet like two cones tip to tip, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></semantics></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> m/s. The quasiparticles there obey the massless Dirac equation: their speed does not depend on energy, as for photons, and the effective mass has to be replaced by the cyclotron mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, which varies with carrier density as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>. In 2005 Novoselov and co-workers measured this <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math> dependence and an anomalous quantum Hall effect, confirming that electrons in graphene are “massless Dirac fermions” <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-novoselov2005\" data-ref=\"novoselov2005\">Novoselov et al. 2005</a>]</span>."},{"note":"<b>Effective mass does not mean the “real mass changed”.</b> The free electron’s mass is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> in every material. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> describes how the electron <b>plus</b> its interaction with the lattice responds to external forces. There is more clothing to come: coupling to phonons makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> heavier still (<a href=\"#c-polarons\" data-open=\"polarons\">07 · Polarons: an electron dressed in the lattice</a>), and correlations among electrons can make it hundreds of times heavier (<a href=\"#c-fermi\" data-open=\"fermi\">04 · Fermi liquids: why strongly interacting electrons still look free</a>).","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"Bloch states","p":"Eigenstates in a periodic potential are labelled by crystal momentum ħk and a band index, their energies forming bands.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Effective mass = band curvature","p":"1/m* = (1/ħ²)∂²E/∂k²; m* is negative at the top of a band.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Full bands carry no current","p":"So conduction in an almost full band is set by the empty states: holes with charge +e and positive mass.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Hall coefficient and cyclotron resonance","p":"The sign of the Hall voltage measures carrier charge and cyclotron resonance measures m*; germanium has heavy and light holes.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Massless Dirac fermions","p":"Graphene’s linear dispersion at K; cyclotron mass ∝ √n.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">R</mi></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Bloch’s theorem."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">E</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Semiclassical equations of motion."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">J</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mtext> ⁣</mtext><munder><mo>∑</mo><mtext>occupied</mtext></munder><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mtext> ⁣</mtext><munder><mo>∑</mo><mtext>empty</mtext></munder><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Because Σ_all v = 0, the current can be counted with electrons or with holes."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>e</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Hall coefficient with two carrier types: the sign follows the majority carrier."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Graphene’s Dirac cone and cyclotron mass (spin and valley degeneracy 4)."}],"example":{"q":"A one-dimensional tight-binding band <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> eV and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> nm. What is the effective mass at the band bottom? What is the cyclotron mass in graphene at a carrier density of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>12</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> cm⁻² (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> m/s)?","steps":["Near the bottom <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0762</mn></mrow></semantics></math> eV·nm², <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0762</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.09</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.42</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","Graphene: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>16</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> m⁻², <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>1.77</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> m⁻¹, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.055</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>34</mn></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>1.77</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.87</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> kg <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.021</mn><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>."],"a":"About <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.42</mn><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> at the bottom of the tight-binding band; graphene’s cyclotron mass is only about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.02</mn><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, and it doubles only when the density quadruples."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-hall1879\" data-ref=\"hall1879\">Hall 1879</a> · <a href=\"#ref-haynes1951\" data-ref=\"haynes1951\">Haynes &amp; Shockley 1951</a> · <a href=\"#ref-dresselhaus1955\" data-ref=\"dresselhaus1955\">Dresselhaus, Kip &amp; Kittel 1955</a> · <a href=\"#ref-novoselov2005\" data-ref=\"novoselov2005\">Novoselov et al. 2005</a>"},"fermi":{"id":"fermi","body":[{"p":"A conduction electron in copper has a kinetic energy of a few eV, and its Coulomb energy with neighbouring electrons is also a few eV. On paper this is a strongly interacting system. Yet the free-electron model describes the heat capacity, conductivity and susceptibility of metals astonishingly well: the heat capacity is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> only twenty or thirty per cent above the free-electron value. Why?"},{"h":"Landau’s argument"},{"p":"Landau’s answer in 1956 was “adiabatic continuity”: start from the free Fermi gas and switch the interaction on slowly. As long as no phase transition occurs along the way, every low-energy excitation of the free gas (adding a particle outside the Fermi surface, removing one from inside) turns continuously into an excitation of the interacting system. These are still labelled by momentum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, are still spin-½ fermions, are as numerous as the free electrons, and enclose the same Fermi-surface volume (Luttinger’s theorem). What changes is their dispersion (effective mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math>) and a small residual interaction among them, described by a set of Landau parameters <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"p":"The key to the argument is lifetime. For a quasiparticle at energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (measured from the Fermi surface) to decay, it must share its energy with another electron below the Fermi surface, producing two quasiparticles and one hole. The Pauli principle confines all three energies to between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, so the phase space is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, and the decay rate is"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>τ</mi></mfrac><mo>∝</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"It shrinks faster than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi></mrow></semantics></math> itself: the closer to the Fermi surface, the smaller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>ε</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and the “better” the quasiparticle. Excitations far from the Fermi surface decay quickly, so Fermi-liquid theory is only a low-energy theory, but the thermodynamics and transport of a metal care precisely about excitations within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math> of the Fermi surface. With interactions the momentum distribution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> is no longer a step from 1 to 0, but it still jumps at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, by exactly the quasiparticle weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"demo":"fermi"},{"h":"Measurable consequences"},{"list":["<b>Heat capacity</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math>: measuring <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> measures the effective mass. Liquid helium-3 is the cleanest Fermi liquid, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> at zero pressure.","<b>Resistivity</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>: the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> comes from the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> above. In ordinary metals it is masked by phonons; it shows clearly at low temperature and in strongly correlated materials.","<b>Zero sound.</b> A neutral Fermi liquid supports another collective excitation, restored not by collisions but by the Landau interaction among quasiparticles; it has been observed in helium-3. In a charged electron liquid the corresponding collective mode is the plasmon (<a href=\"#c-plasmons\" data-open=\"plasmons\">08 · Plasmons: the electron sea oscillating as a whole</a>)."]},{"h":"Heavy fermions"},{"p":"How far can the Fermi-liquid picture stretch? Some compounds containing cerium, ytterbium or uranium (CeAl₃, CeCu₆, UPt₃ and others) give a startling answer: at low temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> reaches several hundred to over a thousand mJ/(mol·K²), a thousand times that of copper, corresponding to quasiparticles hundreds of times heavier than electrons, yet the heat capacity, susceptibility and resistivity still follow Fermi-liquid laws <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-stewart1984\" data-ref=\"stewart1984\">Stewart 1984</a>]</span>. The local f-electron moments and the conduction electrons “hybridise” at low temperature into an extremely narrow band, and the quasiparticles live in it. Kadowaki and Woods found that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> in these materials obey a universal relation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mtext> </mtext><mtext>cm</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mtext>mol</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext>K/mJ</mtext></mrow><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kadowaki1986\" data-ref=\"kadowaki1986\">Kadowaki &amp; Woods 1986</a>]</span>: the heavier the mass, the stronger the scattering, both coming from the same narrow band."},{"h":"When the Fermi liquid fails"},{"p":"Tune a heavy-fermion material to the edge of a magnetic phase transition (a quantum critical point) and the Fermi liquid starts to fail: the resistivity becomes linear in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math> diverges as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, as if the quasiparticle mass went to infinity <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-stewart2001\" data-ref=\"stewart2001\">Stewart 2001</a>]</span>. The normal state of the high-temperature cuprate superconductors also has a “strange metal” region with a linear resistivity over a wide temperature range. The “marginal Fermi liquid” in the demo is a phenomenological model proposed for such behaviour: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mo>∝</mo><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>ε</mi></mrow></semantics></math> no longer vanishes at the Fermi surface and quasiparticles barely exist. How to describe a metal with no quasiparticles at all is among the biggest open problems of condensed-matter physics."}],"points":[{"h":"Adiabatic continuity","p":"Without a phase transition the low-energy excitations of the free Fermi gas map one to one onto quasiparticles; the Fermi-surface volume is unchanged (Luttinger).","st":"approx"},{"h":"Γ ∝ ε² + (πk_BT)²","p":"The Pauli principle limits phase space, so lifetimes grow towards the Fermi surface.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The jump in n(k)","p":"With interactions n(k) still jumps by Z at k_F.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Heavy fermions","p":"Still Fermi liquids with γ a thousand times that of copper; A/γ² is roughly universal (Kadowaki–Woods).","st":"tested"},{"h":"Non-Fermi liquids","p":"Near quantum critical points and in strange metals resistivity ∝ T; how to describe metals without quasiparticles remains open.","st":"open"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi>σ</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>V</mi></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi>σ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></munder><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi>σ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Landau’s energy functional: quasiparticle energies plus the residual interaction f."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>3</mn></mfrac><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>N</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>per volume</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Effective mass and heat-capacity coefficient (3D, Galilean-invariant liquid)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>χ</mi><msub><mi>χ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>a</mi></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The spin susceptibility is enhanced or reduced by F₀ᵃ."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mfrac><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mtext> </mtext><mtext>cm</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mtext>mol</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mtext>K/mJ</mtext></mrow><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Fermi-liquid resistivity and the Kadowaki–Woods relation."}],"example":{"q":"A heavy-fermion compound has a heat-capacity coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>400</mn></mrow></semantics></math> mJ/(mol·K²). Estimate its <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></semantics></math> from the Kadowaki–Woods relation. What is the Fermi-liquid part of the resistivity at 2 K? Copper has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow></semantics></math> mJ/(mol·K²); roughly how much heavier are this material’s quasiparticles?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>40</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.6</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mtext>cm/K</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.6</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6.4</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mtext> </mtext><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow></semantics></math>, the same order as copper’s total room-temperature resistivity (1.7 μΩ·cm), yet coming only from electron–electron scattering at 2 K.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> (for similar carrier densities), so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>400</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.7</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>570</mn></mrow></semantics></math>."],"a":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.6</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mtext>cm/K</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, about 6 μΩ·cm at 2 K; the quasiparticles are several hundred times heavier than copper’s electrons (roughly 570 times, to be corrected for different carrier densities)."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-stewart1984\" data-ref=\"stewart1984\">Stewart 1984</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kadowaki1986\" data-ref=\"kadowaki1986\">Kadowaki &amp; Woods 1986</a> · <a href=\"#ref-stewart2001\" data-ref=\"stewart2001\">Stewart 2001</a>"},"magnons":{"id":"magnons","body":[{"p":"Take the simplest ferromagnet: the Heisenberg model <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>J</mi><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, whose ground state has every spin up. Flip one spin down and its exchange energy with both neighbours gets worse, costing energy of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>J</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. But if the “flip” is spread out as a wave, each spin tilting only slightly and precessing about the vertical one after another, neighbouring spins differ by a small angle and the cost is far lower. The longer the wavelength, the smaller the angle and the lower the energy. This is the <b>spin wave</b> Bloch proposed in 1930 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bloch1930\" data-ref=\"bloch1930\">Bloch 1930</a>]</span>."},{"demo":"magnon"},{"p":"Quantum mechanically a spin wave’s energy also comes in portions, and each portion is a <b>magnon</b>. It carries spin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (lowering the total spin by one unit) and is a boson. On a one-dimensional spin-½ ferromagnetic chain the one-magnon states are exact eigenstates of the Hamiltonian, with dispersion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>J</mi><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> and no approximation at all; the exact diagonalisation in the demo lands precisely on the curve. Several magnons interact with each other; in 1940 Holstein and Primakoff wrote the spin operators as an expansion in boson operators <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-holstein1940\" data-ref=\"holstein1940\">Holstein &amp; Primakoff 1940</a>]</span>, whose lowest order is a gas of independent magnons and whose higher terms describe their collisions."},{"h":"Ferro- and antiferromagnets"},{"p":"Ferromagnetic magnons have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>≈</mo><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> at long wavelength, like a massive particle; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is the spin-wave stiffness. Antiferromagnets are quite different: neighbouring spins are antiparallel and linear spin-wave theory gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>∝</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, linear like phonons, with two degenerate branches. The difference comes from symmetry: a ferromagnet’s order parameter (the total magnetisation) is itself conserved, an antiferromagnet’s is not."},{"p":"The one-dimensional spin-½ antiferromagnetic chain is a reminder: there is no long-range order, and the true excitations are spin-½ “spinons”, so one spin flip splits into two of them; neutron scattering sees a continuum rather than a sharp magnon line. What the quasiparticles are depends on what the ground state is."},{"h":"Bloch’s T^{3/2} law"},{"p":"Each magnon lowers the total spin by one unit. As the temperature rises there are more and more thermal magnons and the magnetisation falls. In a three-dimensional ferromagnet the long-wave magnons have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> and a density of states <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∝</mo><msqrt><mi>ω</mi></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>, and summing the Bose distribution gives a magnon number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo></mrow></semantics></math>: Bloch’s law. It differs sharply from the exponential decrease predicted by mean-field theory, and experiment sides with Bloch."},{"h":"Seeing magnons"},{"p":"Neutrons have a magnetic moment, and when scattered by a magnetic material they can emit or absorb a magnon, just as with phonons. In 1957 Brockhouse first detected spin waves with neutrons, in magnetite <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-brockhouse1957\" data-ref=\"brockhouse1957\">Brockhouse 1957</a>]</span>; inelastic neutron scattering has since become the standard way to measure magnon dispersions, from which the exchange constants <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi></mrow></semantics></math> can be extracted. Low-energy long-wave magnons can also be measured by ferromagnetic resonance (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>) and Brillouin light scattering."},{"h":"Condensates and information"},{"p":"Magnons are bosons, but their number is usually not conserved; like phonons their chemical potential is zero and they do not condense on their own. In yttrium iron garnet (YIG), however, an insulating ferromagnet with extremely low damping, magnons exchange energy far faster than they decay. Pump in a large number with microwaves and they first reach a “quasi-equilibrium” with a non-zero chemical potential, then Bose–Einstein condense at the bottom of the band, and at room temperature <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-demokritov2006\" data-ref=\"demokritov2006\">Demokritov et al. 2006</a>]</span>. Magnons can also carry spin information without moving charge, and so without Ohmic heating: this is “magnonics”, a branch of spintronics <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-chumak2015\" data-ref=\"chumak2015\">Chumak et al. 2015</a>]</span>."}],"points":[{"h":"Spin waves","p":"Spreading one spin flip over all the spins as a wave; its energy goes to zero at long wavelength.","st":"proved"},{"h":"One-magnon states are exact eigenstates","p":"On a ferromagnetic Heisenberg chain ħω = 2JS(1 − cos ka) is exact.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Ferro k², antiferro |k|","p":"The ferromagnetic order parameter is conserved and the dispersion is quadratic; the antiferromagnetic one is linear (linear spin-wave theory).","st":"approx"},{"h":"Bloch’s law","p":"In a 3D ferromagnet at low temperature M(0) − M(T) ∝ T^{3/2}.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Neutrons measure the dispersion","p":"First detected in magnetite in 1957; exchange constants are extracted from the dispersion.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Room-temperature magnon BEC and magnonics","p":"Pumped magnons condense at room temperature in YIG; carrying information with magnons is an active research field.","st":"frontier"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>S</mi></mrow></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>S</mi></mrow></mfrac><msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>z</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Holstein–Primakoff transformation."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>J</mi><mi>S</mi><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">δ</mi></munder><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">δ</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>J</mi><mi>S</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>simple cubic</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Ferromagnetic magnon dispersion and spin-wave stiffness."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>J</mi><mi>S</mi><mi>z</mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>z</mi></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">δ</mi></munder><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">δ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Linear spin-wave dispersion of an antiferromagnet (z is the coordination number)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mi>S</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Bloch’s T^{3/2} law (simple cubic)."}],"example":{"q":"Iron has a spin-wave stiffness of about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>280</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV·Å². What is the energy of a magnon with wavevector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Å⁻¹? What is the typical wavevector of thermal magnons at 10 K?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>280</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2.8</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV.","At 10 K, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.862</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV. Setting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>0.862</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>280</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>0.055</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Å⁻¹, a wavelength <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></semantics></math> nm, about 40 lattice constants."],"a":"2.8 meV; thermal magnons at 10 K have wavelengths of about 11 nm, much longer than the atomic spacing, so the continuum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> description is entirely adequate and Bloch’s law holds."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-bloch1930\" data-ref=\"bloch1930\">Bloch 1930</a> · <a href=\"#ref-holstein1940\" data-ref=\"holstein1940\">Holstein &amp; Primakoff 1940</a> · <a href=\"#ref-brockhouse1957\" data-ref=\"brockhouse1957\">Brockhouse 1957</a> · <a href=\"#ref-demokritov2006\" data-ref=\"demokritov2006\">Demokritov et al. 2006</a> · <a href=\"#ref-chumak2015\" data-ref=\"chumak2015\">Chumak et al. 2015</a>"},"excitons":{"id":"excitons","body":[{"p":"Single-electron band theory says photons below the gap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> are not absorbed. Yet experiments often show sharp absorption lines below the gap. The reason is the Coulomb attraction between electron and hole: the two-particle state “an electron in the conduction band and a hole in the valence band” can have bound states below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. Frenkel first proposed such “excitation waves” in 1931: the excitation does not stay on one atom but propagates through the crystal site by site <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-frenkel1931\" data-ref=\"frenkel1931\">Frenkel 1931</a>]</span>."},{"h":"Frenkel and Wannier"},{"list":["<b>Frenkel excitons</b>: electron and hole on the same molecule or atom, with a radius smaller than the lattice constant and a large binding energy (0.1 to over 1 eV). Found in molecular crystals (anthracene, pentacene), alkali halides, rare-gas solids and the light-harvesting complexes of photosynthesis. They move by the excitation “hopping” from one molecule to the next.","<b>Wannier excitons</b> (Wannier–Mott excitons): electron and hole are many lattice constants apart, and the crystal between them can be treated as a continuum with dielectric constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wannier1937\" data-ref=\"wannier1937\">Wannier 1937</a>]</span>. This is a hydrogen atom, only with the reduced mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and a Coulomb force weakened by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Typical of inorganic semiconductors (GaAs, CdS, Cu₂O)."]},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>13.6</mn><mtext> eV</mtext><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>0.0529</mn><mtext> nm</mtext></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"In GaAs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.06</mn><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, so the Rydberg energy is only a few meV and the radius over ten nanometres, more than twenty lattice constants, making the continuum assumption excellent. At room temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>26</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV, far above the binding energy, so excitons are quickly torn apart and GaAs excitons must be observed at low temperature. The demo below draws the full absorption spectrum from the Elliott formula: a series of lines crowding towards the gap from below, and the continuum above the gap raised by the Coulomb attraction."},{"demo":"exciton"},{"h":"Giant Rydberg excitons"},{"p":"Cuprous oxide Cu₂O is the classic material of exciton physics: its “yellow series” has a Rydberg energy of about 92 meV and very narrow levels. In 2014 Kazimierczuk and co-workers saw exciton lines all the way up to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow></semantics></math> in a natural Cu₂O crystal; that exciton is about 2 micrometres across, larger than a bacterium <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kazimierczuk2014\" data-ref=\"kazimierczuk2014\">Kazimierczuk et al. 2014</a>]</span>. Such large excitons show strong “Rydberg blockade”: while one exists, a second cannot be excited nearby, just as with Rydberg atoms in cold-atom physics."},{"h":"Two-dimensional materials: bound especially tightly"},{"p":"Make a semiconductor thinner and its excitons bind more tightly. An ideal two-dimensional hydrogen atom has four times the binding energy of the three-dimensional one (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>). More important is screening: a monolayer has vacuum or a thin substrate above and below, so most of the field lines run outside where the dielectric constant is small, and the Coulomb attraction is hardly weakened. MoS₂ becomes a direct-gap semiconductor at monolayer thickness, with luminescence enhanced by a factor of a thousand or more <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-mak2010\" data-ref=\"mak2010\">Mak et al. 2010</a>]</span>; monolayer WS₂ has an exciton binding energy of about 0.32 eV, ten times that of ordinary semiconductors, and its lines do not follow the hydrogenic <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> law because the effective screening varies with distance <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-chernikov2014\" data-ref=\"chernikov2014\">Chernikov et al. 2014</a>]</span>. These excitons are stable at room temperature and dominate the optics of monolayer transition-metal dichalcogenides <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wang2018\" data-ref=\"wang2018\">Wang et al. 2018</a>]</span>."},{"h":"What excitons do"},{"p":"Excitons are neutral and carry no current, but they carry energy and diffuse through the material. In organic solar cells light first creates Frenkel excitons, which must diffuse to the interface between two materials before splitting into electron and hole; exciton diffusion lengths are only a few to tens of nanometres, so the active layer of an organic cell is made as an interpenetrating nanoscale structure. Light-emitting diodes run the other way: electrons and holes meet to form excitons, which recombine and emit photons. In the light-harvesting antennae of photosynthesis, excitation also moves between pigment molecules as excitons (the exciton-polaritons of <a href=\"#c-polaritons\" data-open=\"polaritons\">09 · Polaritons: half light, half matter</a> are what results when excitons mix further with light)."}],"points":[{"h":"Absorption lines below the gap","p":"The Coulomb attraction between electron and hole produces bound states below E_g.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Frenkel and Wannier","p":"Small, tight Frenkel excitons (molecular crystals) and large, loose Wannier excitons (inorganic semiconductors).","st":"tested"},{"h":"The hydrogen model","p":"Ry* = 13.6 eV · μ/ε², a* = 0.053 nm · ε/μ; valid only when the radius greatly exceeds the lattice constant.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Rydberg excitons","p":"n = 25 seen in Cu₂O, about 2 μm across, with Rydberg blockade.","st":"tested"},{"h":"2D excitons","p":"Excitons in monolayer TMDs bind by hundreds of meV, and their series departs from 1/n².","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">[</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>μ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">]</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>μ</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Wannier equation: relative motion is hydrogenic, and the centre of mass moves freely with the total mass."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∝</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></munder><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi>δ</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>π</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Elliott formula, x = √(Ry*/(ħω − E_g))."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>D</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Ideal 2D exciton levels: the ground-state binding is 4 times the 3D value."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>8</mn><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">[</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">]</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Rytova–Keldysh potential in monolayers (r₀ the screening length, κ the mean dielectric constant of the surroundings): logarithmic at short range, 1/r at long range."}],"example":{"q":"The yellow series of Cu₂O has a Rydberg energy of about 92 meV and an exciton Bohr radius of about 1.1 nm. Estimate the size of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow></semantics></math> exciton and how far its level lies below the gap.","steps":["A hydrogen atom’s mean radius is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> (a little less for high angular momentum). For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>625</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1.1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> nm <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> μm, a diameter of about 2 μm.","Distance below the gap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>92</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>625</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.15</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV, equivalent to 1.7 K. Neighbouring lines (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and 25) differ by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.012</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV."],"a":"About 2 μm across and only about 0.15 meV below the gap. To resolve these lines the sample must be very pure, the temperature around 1 K, and the laser linewidth narrow to the μeV level."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-frenkel1931\" data-ref=\"frenkel1931\">Frenkel 1931</a> · <a href=\"#ref-wannier1937\" data-ref=\"wannier1937\">Wannier 1937</a> · <a href=\"#ref-mak2010\" data-ref=\"mak2010\">Mak et al. 2010</a> · <a href=\"#ref-chernikov2014\" data-ref=\"chernikov2014\">Chernikov et al. 2014</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kazimierczuk2014\" data-ref=\"kazimierczuk2014\">Kazimierczuk et al. 2014</a> · <a href=\"#ref-wang2018\" data-ref=\"wang2018\">Wang et al. 2018</a>"},"polarons":{"id":"polarons","body":[{"p":"Put an extra electron into an ionic crystal such as NaCl. It pulls nearby Na⁺ towards itself and pushes Cl⁻ away, polarising the lattice locally, and the polarisation in turn produces a potential well that attracts the electron where it sits. The slower the electron, the better the lattice keeps up and the deeper the well. The electron together with the polarisation it drags along is the <b>polaron</b>. Landau proposed the idea in 1933, and Pekar developed it into a strong-coupling theory in the 1940s."},{"h":"The Fröhlich model"},{"p":"In 1954 Fröhlich wrote down the standard model of the “large polaron”: an electron in a continuum, coupled to a single dispersionless branch of longitudinal optical phonons at frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-froehlich1954\" data-ref=\"froehlich1954\">Fröhlich 1954</a>]</span>. The strength of the coupling is set by one dimensionless number:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi></mrow></mfrac><msqrt><mfrac><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> are the high-frequency and static dielectric constants, and their difference is the part of the polarisation the ions can follow. GaAs has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.07</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, very weak; alkali halides lie between 3 and 5; some oxides are larger."},{"p":"At weak coupling perturbation theory works: the electron keeps emitting and reabsorbing virtual phonons, its energy falls by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and its mass becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. At strong coupling the electron digs itself a pit whose shape and the electron’s wavefunction determine each other (Pekar), with energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>≈</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.1085</mn><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and a mass growing as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>. Both ends are clear; what about the middle?"},{"h":"Feynman’s variational solution"},{"p":"In 1955 Feynman used his newly invented path integrals to give an answer valid for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-feynman1955\" data-ref=\"feynman1955\">Feynman 1955</a>]</span>. First integrate out the phonons, leaving a time-non-local attraction between the electron and “its own past positions”; then approximate this by an exactly solvable model in which the electron is tied by a spring to a fictitious mass, with the two parameters <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></semantics></math> tuned to minimise an upper bound on the energy. The bound comes close to exact at both weak and strong coupling, with errors of only about one per cent in between, as later numerical methods (diffusion and diagrammatic Monte Carlo) confirmed. The demo below performs this variation on the spot."},{"demo":"polaron"},{"h":"Large and small polarons"},{"p":"The Fröhlich model assumes a distortion much larger than the lattice constant: the “large polaron”. If the coupling to a local distortion is strong enough and the band narrow, the electron can be trapped on <b>one</b> site (or one bond), with clearly displaced atoms around it: the <b>small polaron</b> (the situation the Holstein model describes). Small polarons move in a completely different way: not at a band velocity but by thermally activated hops from site to site, so their mobility <b>rises</b> with temperature. In transition-metal oxides (TiO₂, Fe₂O₃, BiVO₄), organic semiconductors and halide perovskites polarons are key to understanding carrier behaviour, and they can now be computed directly from first principles <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-franchini2021\" data-ref=\"franchini2021\">Franchini et al. 2021</a>]</span>."},{"note":"<b>Polarons are not only about electrons and phonons.</b> Whenever a particle drags a cloud of disturbance through a medium, the polaron idea applies: “magnetic polarons” dragging spin polarisation in magnets, and impurity atoms immersed in a Bose–Einstein condensate of cold atoms (“Bose polarons”), are treated with the same mathematics.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"Self-trapping","p":"The electron polarises the lattice and the polarisation attracts the electron; electron plus distortion is the polaron.","st":"tested"},{"h":"One coupling constant α","p":"The Fröhlich model has only α: about 0.07 for GaAs, 3–5 for alkali halides.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Weak and strong coupling","p":"Weak coupling E ≈ −αħω, m* ≈ 1 + α/6; strong coupling (Pekar) E ≈ −0.1085α²ħω.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Feynman’s variation","p":"Path integrals give an upper bound on the energy for every α, accurate to about 1 %.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Small-polaron hopping","p":"With strong localisation the mobility rises with temperature (activated hopping); common in oxides and organic semiconductors.","st":"tested"},{"h":"First-principles polarons","p":"Computing polaron shapes and energies directly from electron–phonon coupling is developing fast.","st":"frontier"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></munder><mfrac><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>q</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi></msub><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mtext>h.c.</mtext><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>∝</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Fröhlich Hamiltonian."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>w</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>v</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow><msqrt><mi>π</mi></msqrt></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>v</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>τ</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Feynman’s variational bound (units ħ = ω_LO = m* = 1)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mn>6</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mo>…</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.0200</mn><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The two limits of the polaron mass."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>hop</mtext></msub><mo>∝</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Small-polaron hopping mobility (non-adiabatic Marcus–Holstein form, E_b the polaron binding energy)."}],"example":{"q":"GaAs has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.068</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>36</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV; an alkali halide has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV. Estimate the polaron binding energy and mass increase for each.","steps":["GaAs: weak coupling, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mi>α</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.068</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>36</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2.4</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.068</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.011</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, only 1 % heavier.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: perturbation theory is no longer reliable, so use Feynman’s result (slide the demo to 3): <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3.133</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.89</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. The binding energy is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.133</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>78</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV."],"a":"About 2.4 meV and 1 % heavier for GaAs; about 78 meV and nearly twice as heavy for the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> ionic crystal. The latter’s binding is three times room-temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, so polaron effects must be included."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-froehlich1954\" data-ref=\"froehlich1954\">Fröhlich 1954</a> · <a href=\"#ref-feynman1955\" data-ref=\"feynman1955\">Feynman 1955</a> · <a href=\"#ref-franchini2021\" data-ref=\"franchini2021\">Franchini et al. 2021</a>"},"plasmons":{"id":"plasmons","body":[{"p":"Shift all the conduction electrons of a metal rightwards by a small distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. A layer of positive charge is exposed on the left and an excess of negative charge appears on the right, producing a field <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> in between that pulls the electrons back. It is a spring, with a restoring force proportional to the displacement, so the electron sea oscillates at a fixed frequency:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>37.1</mn><mtext> eV</mtext><mo>×</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>24</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mtext>cm</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>⋅</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mfrac></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"The frequency hardly depends on wavelength, and the oscillation involves every electron. Once quantised, one portion of this oscillation is a <b>bulk plasmon</b>. Metals have electron densities of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>22</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>23</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> cm⁻³, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> between 5 and 20 eV, far above room-temperature thermal energy, so plasmons are not thermally excited and sit in their ground state."},{"h":"Collective coordinates and screening"},{"p":"In a series of papers from 1951 to 1953 Bohm and Pines turned this intuition into a theory <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bohm1951\" data-ref=\"bohm1951\">Bohm &amp; Pines 1951</a>; <a href=\"#ref-pines1952\" data-ref=\"pines1952\">Pines &amp; Bohm 1952</a>]</span>: the long-range Coulomb interaction between electrons can be split in two. Its long-wavelength part becomes a few collective degrees of freedom, the plasmons, whose energy is so high that they cannot be excited at low energy; what remains is a short-range, screened interaction. So at low energy the electrons look like weakly interacting quasiparticles, one reason the Fermi liquid of <a href=\"#c-fermi\" data-open=\"fermi\">04 · Fermi liquids: why strongly interacting electrons still look free</a> works. Seen from the other side, the plasmon is the electron liquid’s “zero sound”, its frequency lifted from zero to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> by the electrons’ charge."},{"h":"Why metals shine"},{"p":"The electron sea’s response to light is the dielectric function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>γ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> (the Drude model). For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and the refractive index is almost purely imaginary: light cannot enter and is almost totally reflected, which is metallic lustre. Above the plasma frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and the metal becomes transparent. Alkali metals have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> between 4 and 6 eV and are transparent in the ultraviolet; silver’s d electrons raise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> to about 4, putting the reflection edge in the near ultraviolet, so silver is white; gold and copper have d-band absorption in the visible, so they are coloured."},{"demo":"plasmon"},{"p":"Light is a transverse wave and cannot directly excite the bulk plasmon, which is longitudinal. Fast electrons can: an electron crossing a thin film pushes the electron sea with its Coulomb field and leaves behind a portion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> of energy. A series of equally spaced peaks in the electron energy-loss spectrum is one electron exciting one, two, three plasmons in turn. Aluminium’s loss peak is near 15 eV, close to the 15.8 eV computed from its free-electron density."},{"h":"Surface plasmons"},{"p":"A metal surface supports another oscillation. In 1957 Ritchie predicted that thin films support a lower-frequency plasmon at their surfaces, at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> for a flat surface, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ritchie1957\" data-ref=\"ritchie1957\">Ritchie 1957</a>]</span>. Two years later Powell and Swan confirmed, in aluminium’s electron energy-loss spectrum, that this lower peak comes from the surface: oxidise the surface and the peak moves <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-powell1959\" data-ref=\"powell1959\">Powell &amp; Swan 1959</a>]</span>."},{"p":"Coupled to light, the surface plasmon becomes a <b>surface plasmon polariton</b> propagating along the metal surface (itself a kind of polariton, <a href=\"#c-polaritons\" data-open=\"polaritons\">09 · Polaritons: half light, half matter</a>). Its field hugs the surface and decays exponentially on both sides, confining light energy to regions far smaller than its wavelength: the basis of “subwavelength optics” <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-barnes2003\" data-ref=\"barnes2003\">Barnes, Dereux &amp; Ebbesen 2003</a>]</span>. Localised plasmons on small metal particles resonate at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: gold nanoparticles absorb green light near 520 nm and their solutions look red, the effect used in medieval stained glass and in today’s biosensing and surface-enhanced Raman scattering."}],"points":[{"h":"The plasma frequency","p":"A rigid shift of the electron sea produces a linear restoring force and oscillation at ω_p = √(ne²/ε₀m*).","st":"proved"},{"h":"Collective coordinates and screening","p":"The long-wave part of the Coulomb interaction becomes plasmons, leaving a screened short-range interaction (Bohm–Pines).","st":"approx"},{"h":"The reflection edge","p":"For ω &lt; ω_p/√ε∞, ε &lt; 0 and the metal reflects almost totally; alkali metals are transparent in the UV.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Electron energy loss","p":"Fast electrons excite bulk plasmons one portion at a time; about 15 eV for aluminium.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Surface and localised plasmons","p":"At ε = −1 on a flat surface and ε = −2 for a small sphere; they squeeze light below the wavelength.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>γ</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Drude dielectric function."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Bulk plasmon dispersion (long-wave expansion of the random-phase approximation)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>⇒</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Surface plasmon (vacuum interface) and the dipole plasmon of a small sphere (surrounding medium ε_d)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>ω</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Surface plasmon polariton dispersion: its wavevector exceeds that of light at the same frequency."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>P</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>ω</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">[</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">]</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Electron energy-loss spectroscopy measures the loss function."}],"example":{"q":"Aluminium has a valence-electron density of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.81</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>23</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> cm⁻³ (three valence electrons per atom). Compute its bulk and surface plasmon energies. Then find the wavelength of the plasma reflection edge of n-type GaAs doped to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>19</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> cm⁻³ (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.067</mn><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10.9</mn></mrow></semantics></math>).","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>37.1</mn><mtext> eV</mtext><mo>×</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>24</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mtext>cm</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>. Aluminium: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>0.181</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>0.425</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>37.1</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.425</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>15.8</mn></mrow></semantics></math> eV; the surface plasmon is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15.8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>11.2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> eV.","GaAs: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>37.1</mn><mo>×</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.067</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>37.1</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.0122</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.454</mn></mrow></semantics></math> eV, screened to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.454</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mn>10.9</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>0.137</mn></mrow></semantics></math> eV, a wavelength of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1240</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.137</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>9.0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> μm."],"a":"Aluminium 15.8 eV (measured about 15 eV), surface 11.2 eV (measured about 10.5 eV); the doped GaAs edge is in the mid-infrared near 9 μm. Lower the electron density ten-thousand-fold and the plasmon moves from the ultraviolet to the infrared."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-bohm1951\" data-ref=\"bohm1951\">Bohm &amp; Pines 1951</a> · <a href=\"#ref-pines1952\" data-ref=\"pines1952\">Pines &amp; Bohm 1952</a> · <a href=\"#ref-ritchie1957\" data-ref=\"ritchie1957\">Ritchie 1957</a> · <a href=\"#ref-powell1959\" data-ref=\"powell1959\">Powell &amp; Swan 1959</a> · <a href=\"#ref-barnes2003\" data-ref=\"barnes2003\">Barnes, Dereux &amp; Ebbesen 2003</a>"},"polaritons":{"id":"polaritons","body":[{"p":"In vacuum the photon dispersion is a straight line <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. An ionic crystal has a transverse optical phonon branch whose frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> barely changes with wavevector: a horizontal line. The two lines cross near <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. But the optical phonon carries an oscillating electric dipole that couples to the light’s electric field: near the crossing neither “a photon” nor “a phonon” is an eigenstate any more, and the true eigenstates are superpositions with frequencies pushed apart into two branches. This is the <b>phonon-polariton</b>. In 1951 Huang Kun combined the lattice equations of motion with Maxwell’s equations and wrote down its full description <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-huang1951\" data-ref=\"huang1951\">Huang 1951</a>]</span>:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mfrac><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"Between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and no mode propagates: light in this band is almost totally reflected by the crystal, the “reststrahlen” band. The last equality is the Lyddane–Sachs–Teller relation linking the static dielectric constant to the two phonon frequencies. In 1965 Henry and Hopfield measured polariton dispersion in GaP by Raman scattering, seeing the phonon frequency change with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></semantics></math> at small wavevector: direct evidence of light mixing with matter <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-henry1965\" data-ref=\"henry1965\">Henry &amp; Hopfield 1965</a>]</span>."},{"h":"Exciton-polaritons"},{"p":"The same thing happens between light and excitons. In 1958 Hopfield pointed out that near an exciton resonance what propagates in a crystal is neither a photon nor an exciton but a mixture of the two, and he gave the mixing coefficients (now called Hopfield coefficients) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hopfield1958\" data-ref=\"hopfield1958\">Hopfield 1958</a>]</span>. In bulk material exciton-polariton effects are confined to a narrow frequency range; in 1992 Weisbuch and co-workers placed GaAs quantum wells inside a microcavity formed by two Bragg mirrors, trapping photons to interact repeatedly with the excitons, and saw for the first time a clear “vacuum Rabi splitting” in a semiconductor microcavity: strong coupling <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-weisbuch1992\" data-ref=\"weisbuch1992\">Weisbuch et al. 1992</a>]</span>."},{"demo":"polariton"},{"p":"A microcavity photon’s in-plane motion has a “mass” <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, only about four hundred-thousandths of an electron mass; the lower polariton, half light, is roughly twice as heavy and still more than ten thousand times lighter than an electron. It also carries an exciton component, so polaritons collide with each other. Light and collisional means quantum degeneracy at temperatures far higher than for atomic gases. In 2006 Kasprzak and co-workers saw Bose–Einstein condensation of polaritons in a CdTe microcavity: a sharp peak in momentum space, long-range coherence and spontaneous polarisation <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kasprzak2006\" data-ref=\"kasprzak2006\">Kasprzak et al. 2006</a>]</span>. Condensation was later achieved at room temperature in GaN and organic microcavities. Unlike atomic condensates, polaritons leak out of the cavity and must be replenished by continuous pumping: a “driven–dissipative” condensate."},{"h":"Polaritons in two-dimensional materials"},{"p":"In van der Waals materials almost every excitation can mix with light: plasmon-polaritons in graphene, phonon-polaritons in hexagonal boron nitride, exciton-polaritons in transition-metal dichalcogenides, and Dirac plasmons on the surfaces of topological insulators <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-basov2016\" data-ref=\"basov2016\">Basov, Fogler &amp; García de Abajo 2016</a>]</span>. Their wavelengths can be tens to hundreds of times shorter than light at the same frequency, and near-field optical microscopes see their interference fringes directly on the sample surface. Phonon-polaritons in hexagonal boron nitride have a special twist: the dielectric tensor has opposite signs in and out of the plane (a “hyperbolic” material), so light can be squeezed into very thin conical channels."}],"points":[{"h":"Anticrossing","p":"When light couples strongly to a material excitation of the same frequency, the two dispersions repel into two mixed branches.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Huang’s equations and the LST relation","p":"c²k² = ω²ε(ω); ε(0)/ε∞ = ω_L²/ω_T²; the reststrahlen band lies between.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Hopfield coefficients","p":"The light and matter fractions of a polariton vary with detuning and wavevector.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Microcavity strong coupling and condensation","p":"Rabi splitting seen in 1992, Bose–Einstein condensation in 2006, later at room temperature.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Polaritons in 2D materials","p":"Polaritons in graphene, hBN and TMDs compress light far below its wavelength: an active frontier.","st":"frontier"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>E</mi><mo>±</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>±</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The two coupled oscillators model: at k = 0 and zero detuning the splitting is exactly ħΩ."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>X</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>X</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Exciton and photon fractions of the lower polariton (Hopfield coefficients)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mo>∥</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mo>∥</mo></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mo>∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"In-plane dispersion and effective mass of a microcavity photon."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>X</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Lower-polariton mass: excitons are heavy, so the photon part dominates."}],"example":{"q":"A microcavity has photon energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.6</mn></mrow></semantics></math> eV, refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.5</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and zero detuning. What is the lower polariton’s effective mass? What is its thermal de Broglie wavelength at 10 K?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.6</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>12.25</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5.11</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3.84</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. At zero detuning <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>7.7</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>7.0</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>35</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> kg.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>6.63</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>34</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>7.0</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>35</mn></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>1.38</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>22</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>2.7</mn></mrow></semantics></math> μm. A rubidium atom at the same 10 K has only 0.06 nm."],"a":"About <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>; the thermal wavelength at 10 K is about 2.7 μm, more than forty thousand times that of rubidium. Quantum degeneracy needs the interparticle spacing to match the thermal wavelength, so polaritons condense at a few kelvin while atomic gases must be cooled to hundreds of nanokelvin."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-huang1951\" data-ref=\"huang1951\">Huang 1951</a> · <a href=\"#ref-hopfield1958\" data-ref=\"hopfield1958\">Hopfield 1958</a> · <a href=\"#ref-henry1965\" data-ref=\"henry1965\">Henry &amp; Hopfield 1965</a> · <a href=\"#ref-weisbuch1992\" data-ref=\"weisbuch1992\">Weisbuch et al. 1992</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kasprzak2006\" data-ref=\"kasprzak2006\">Kasprzak et al. 2006</a> · <a href=\"#ref-basov2016\" data-ref=\"basov2016\">Basov, Fogler &amp; García de Abajo 2016</a>"},"cooper":{"id":"cooper","body":[{"p":"For more than forty years after superconductivity was discovered in 1911, nobody could explain it microscopically. The isotope effect of 1950 (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>) showed that lattice vibrations take part (see the <a href=\"../quasiparticles/phonons/#c-quantum\">Phonon hall</a>): a passing electron pulls the positive ions closer, the ions are slow to spring back, and the next electron feels a slight attraction. The attraction is weak, and much of it is cancelled by Coulomb repulsion, but in 1956 Cooper proved that as long as a Fermi surface exists, an <b>arbitrarily weak</b> net attraction binds two electrons above it <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-cooper1956\" data-ref=\"cooper1956\">Cooper 1956</a>]</span>."},{"p":"The key is the Fermi surface: the filled states block the two electrons from going lower, so they can only find states in a thin shell above the Fermi surface, a phase space that behaves as if two-dimensional, and in two dimensions any attraction, however weak, has a bound state. The binding energy goes as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the dimensionless coupling), which is “non-analytic” for perturbation theory: precisely why earlier perturbative calculations never found superconductivity."},{"h":"BCS theory"},{"p":"If one pair can bind, what about all the electrons? Bardeen, Cooper and Schrieffer wrote down the answer in 1957: the ground state is a coherent superposition of pairs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>↑</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>↓</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> of opposite momentum and spin, each pair occupied with amplitude <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and empty with amplitude <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bcs1957\" data-ref=\"bcs1957\">Bardeen, Cooper &amp; Schrieffer 1957</a>]</span>. This state does not fix the total particle number but has a definite phase, the origin of superconductivity (zero resistance and the Meissner effect). The gap is set by a self-consistency equation:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><mi>tanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mi>T</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mi>E</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>ξ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mo>⇒</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>sinh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>3.528</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"The final ratio is independent of the material: a universal prediction of weak-coupling BCS theory. The gap grows from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> like <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math> and is already close to its zero-temperature value by half of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"h":"Bogoliubov quasiparticles"},{"p":"In the superconducting ground state the electrons are all paired, so what is the lowest excitation? In 1958 Bogoliubov and Valatin independently gave a linear transformation that mixes an electron’s creation operator with <b>another</b> electron’s annihilation operator, i.e. the creation of a hole <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bogoliubov1958\" data-ref=\"bogoliubov1958\">Bogoliubov 1958</a>]</span>:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>↑</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>↑</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>↓</mo></mrow></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>±</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> creates a <b>Bogoliubov quasiparticle</b>. Far above the Fermi surface <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and it is an electron; far below, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and it is a hole; at the Fermi surface it is half electron and half hole, with zero expected charge. Its energy never falls below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: the zero-energy excitations at the Fermi surface have been pushed up by a gap. The quasiparticles are still fermions, in one-to-one correspondence with the Fermi-liquid quasiparticles of <a href=\"#c-fermi\" data-open=\"fermi\">04 · Fermi liquids: why strongly interacting electrons still look free</a>, only with a changed dispersion."},{"demo":"bcs"},{"p":"The gap empties the density of states for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, and the displaced states pile up at the gap edges in peaks of the form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>. In 1960 Giaever measured current–voltage curves of aluminium–aluminium oxide–lead tunnel junctions: below the voltage corresponding to the gap electrons could not tunnel and the current was nearly zero, then it rose steeply past the gap <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-giaever1960\" data-ref=\"giaever1960\">Giaever 1960</a>]</span>. This was the first direct measurement of the gap; scanning tunnelling spectroscopy later brought it to atomic resolution."},{"h":"Beyond BCS"},{"p":"Lead and mercury have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> between 4.3 and 4.6, above 3.53, because the electron–phonon coupling is too strong and the phonon frequencies too low; they need Eliashberg theory, which includes retardation. Pairing in the cuprates and iron-based superconductors is probably not phonon-mediated, and the cuprate gap has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></semantics></math>-wave symmetry, vanishing along certain directions. Hydrides under high pressure (H₃S superconducting near 200 K, for example) push the electron–phonon mechanism to the extreme. The “half electron, half hole” nature of Bogoliubov quasiparticles is pushed to its own extreme in topological superconductors: a zero-energy Bogoliubov quasiparticle is its own antiparticle, the Majorana zero mode of <a href=\"#c-anyons\" data-open=\"anyons\">11 · Anyons and Majoranas: quasiparticles that exist only in two dimensions</a>."}],"points":[{"h":"The Cooper instability","p":"With a Fermi surface any weak net attraction binds two electrons; the binding ∝ e^{−2/λ} is invisible to perturbation theory.","st":"proved"},{"h":"BCS ground state and gap equation","p":"A coherent superposition of all pairs; 2Δ(0)/k_BT_c = 3.528 (weak coupling, mean field).","st":"approx"},{"h":"Bogoliubov quasiparticles","p":"A superposition of electron and hole with energy √(ξ² + Δ²) ≥ Δ; zero expected charge at the Fermi surface.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Tunnelling sees the gap","p":"Giaever’s 1960 tunnel junctions; density-of-states peaks at the gap edges.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Strong coupling and unconventional pairing","p":"Lead and mercury need Eliashberg theory; cuprates are d-wave, and their pairing mechanism is still debated.","st":"open"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∏</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></munder><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>↑</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>↓</mo></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The BCS wavefunction."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Bogoliubov–de Gennes Hamiltonian: particles and holes written together."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The quasiparticle density of states of a superconductor."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.134</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.74</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mtext>  </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Weak-coupling transition temperature and the gap near T_c."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The coherence length: the size of a Cooper pair."}],"example":{"q":"Aluminium has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.18</mn></mrow></semantics></math> K and lead <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>7.2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> K. Estimate the gaps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> of both from weak-coupling BCS. Lead’s measured value is about 2.7 meV: how much larger than the estimate? Aluminium has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> m/s: how big is a Cooper pair?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.53</mn><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0862</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV/K. Aluminium: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.53</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.0862</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1.18</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.36</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV (measured about 0.34 meV). Lead: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.53</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.0862</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>7.2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2.19</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV; the measured 2.7 meV is 23 % larger, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>4.35</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: strong coupling.","Aluminium: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.18</mn></mrow></semantics></math> meV <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2.9</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> J, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.055</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>34</mn></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>6</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>2.9</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2.3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> m."],"a":"0.36 meV for aluminium and 2.2 meV for lead (2.7 meV measured: strong coupling). An aluminium Cooper pair is about 2 μm across (the literature value is about 1.6 μm), and millions of other pairs overlap within its extent: Cooper pairs are not independent “molecules” but a highly overlapping condensate."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-cooper1956\" data-ref=\"cooper1956\">Cooper 1956</a> · <a href=\"#ref-bcs1957\" data-ref=\"bcs1957\">Bardeen, Cooper &amp; Schrieffer 1957</a> · <a href=\"#ref-bogoliubov1958\" data-ref=\"bogoliubov1958\">Bogoliubov 1958</a> · <a href=\"#ref-giaever1960\" data-ref=\"giaever1960\">Giaever 1960</a>"},"anyons":{"id":"anyons","body":[{"p":"Why only bosons and fermions? In three dimensions, a path taking particle 1 once round particle 2 can be shrunk continuously to a point, so going round once is the same as doing nothing; two exchanges make one full loop, so the exchange phase <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> must satisfy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. In 1977 Leinaas and Myrheim pointed out that this argument fails in two dimensions: a loop round another particle in a plane cannot be shrunk away (the particle in the middle is in the way), so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> can take any value <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-leinaas1977\" data-ref=\"leinaas1977\">Leinaas &amp; Myrheim 1977</a>]</span>. In 1982 Wilczek named such particles “anyons” and built an example from a charged particle carrying a magnetic flux <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-wilczek1982\" data-ref=\"wilczek1982\">Wilczek 1982</a>]</span>."},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">{</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.36em\" columnalign=\"left left\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mtext>bosons</mtext></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>π</mi></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mtext>fermions</mtext></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mtext>anything</mtext></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mtext>anyons (2D)</mtext></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"There are no two-dimensional elementary particles in vacuum, but materials contain two-dimensional electron systems whose quasiparticles can be anyons."},{"h":"The fractional quantum Hall effect"},{"p":"In 1982 Tsui, Störmer and Gossard took a two-dimensional electron gas at a GaAs/AlGaAs interface to very strong magnetic fields and very low temperatures and found a plateau in the Hall resistance at Landau-level filling <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-tsui1982\" data-ref=\"tsui1982\">Tsui, Stormer &amp; Gossard 1982</a>]</span>. The integer quantum Hall effect can be explained with single-electron levels; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> cannot: it is the result of strong interactions among the electrons. In 1983 Laughlin wrote down the wavefunction of this state and showed that its excitations are quasiparticles carrying charge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-laughlin1983\" data-ref=\"laughlin1983\">Laughlin 1983</a>]</span>. Theory soon showed that these quasiparticles have exchange phase <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: they are anyons."},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ψ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"How do you measure a fractional charge? Current flows one charge at a time, so it fluctuates: the shot-noise power <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>I</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is proportional to the charge passing each time. In 1997 two groups let quasiparticles tunnel through a narrow constriction in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> samples and found noise corresponding to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-depicciotto1997\" data-ref=\"depicciotto1997\">de-Picciotto et al. 1997</a>; <a href=\"#ref-saminadayar1997\" data-ref=\"saminadayar1997\">Saminadayar et al. 1997</a>]</span>."},{"p":"Fractional statistics is harder to see, because one quasiparticle must actually be taken round another. Two experiments managed it in 2020: Bartolomei and co-workers built an “anyon collider” in which two beams of quasiparticles meet at a beam splitter, and the measured current cross-correlations matched the prediction for fractional statistics <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bartolomei2020\" data-ref=\"bartolomei2020\">Bartolomei et al. 2020</a>]</span>; Nakamura and co-workers used a Fabry–Pérot interferometer in which edge quasiparticles encircle quasiparticles localised inside the interference region, and saw the interference phase jump in steps of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, exactly the braiding phase of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> anyons <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-nakamura2020\" data-ref=\"nakamura2020\">Nakamura et al. 2020</a>]</span>."},{"h":"Non-Abelian anyons and topological quantum computing"},{"p":"Exchanging <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> anyons only multiplies by a phase, and the order of exchanges does not matter: these are “Abelian” anyons. The quasiparticles of some states (candidates include the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> fractional quantum Hall state) are stranger: several quasiparticles at fixed positions can have several degenerate ground states, and exchanging them performs a <b>matrix</b> transformation among those states, with results that depend on the order. These are <b>non-Abelian anyons</b>. Information stored in the degenerate states can be changed only by moving the quasiparticles round each other (braiding), out of reach of local noise. Kitaev proposed using this for error-resistant quantum computing in 2003 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kitaev2003\" data-ref=\"kitaev2003\">Kitaev 2003</a>]</span>, and the review by Nayak and co-workers lays out the route systematically <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-nayak2008\" data-ref=\"nayak2008\">Nayak et al. 2008</a>]</span>."},{"h":"The Kitaev chain and Majorana zero modes"},{"p":"The simplest non-Abelian objects appear in one dimension. In 2001 Kitaev wrote down a toy model of a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></semantics></math>-wave superconducting chain <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kitaev2001\" data-ref=\"kitaev2001\">Kitaev 2001</a>]</span>: in its topological phase each end of the chain hosts a zero-energy Bogoliubov quasiparticle (<a href=\"#c-cooper\" data-open=\"cooper\">10 · Cooper pairs and Bogoliubov quasiparticles: excitations of a superconductor</a>) that is its own antiparticle, a <b>Majorana zero mode</b>. Only the two together form an ordinary fermionic mode, whose being empty or full costs no energy, with the information split between two distant ends. The demo below diagonalises the chain on the spot: in the topological phase (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>μ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow></semantics></math>) two levels appear almost exactly at zero in the middle of the spectrum, with wavefunctions stuck to the ends; the longer the chain, the closer they are to zero."},{"demo":"kitaev"},{"p":"How is it done in real materials? One proposal is to put a semiconductor nanowire with strong spin–orbit coupling (InSb, InAs) on an ordinary superconductor and add a magnetic field. In 2012 Mourik and co-workers saw a zero-bias conductance peak in such devices, consistent with the prediction for Majorana zero modes <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-mourik2012\" data-ref=\"mourik2012\">Mourik et al. 2012</a>]</span>. But zero-bias peaks can also arise from disorder and other “trivial” bound states, and a 2018 paper reporting quantised conductance was later retracted. In 2023 a Microsoft team proposed a stricter “topological gap protocol” and reported devices that pass it <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-aghaee2023\" data-ref=\"aghaee2023\">Aghaee et al. 2023</a>]</span>; these results are also still under discussion. So far there is no accepted experiment braiding Majoranas."},{"note":"<b>Related sections.</b> Topological quantum computing is only one route to quantum hardware; superconducting, trapped-ion and neutral-atom routes and the state of error correction are in <a href=\"../quantum/computing/\">Quantum Carnival · Quantum computers</a>.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"Exchange statistics in 2D","p":"Loops in a plane cannot be shrunk away, so the exchange phase can be anything: anyons.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Fractional charge e/3","p":"The Laughlin quasiparticles of ν = 1/3; shot noise measured e/3 in 1997.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Fractional statistics","p":"The 2020 anyon collider and interferometer saw θ = π/3 (braiding phase 2π/3).","st":"tested"},{"h":"Non-Abelian anyons","p":"Exchanges act as matrices on degenerate ground states; ν = 5/2 is a candidate, not yet confirmed.","st":"frontier"},{"h":"The Kitaev chain","p":"In the topological phase (|μ| &lt; 2t) each end hosts a Majorana zero mode, with splitting falling exponentially with length.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Majoranas in nanowires","p":"Zero-bias peaks have other explanations; one paper was retracted; rigorous evidence and braiding are still lacking.","st":"open"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ν</mi><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>7</mn></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo>…</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Hall conductance of the fractional quantum Hall effect."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mtext>weak backscattering</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Shot noise measures the quasiparticle charge."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>†</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mtext>h.c.</mtext><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mtext>h.c.</mtext><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Kitaev chain."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><msup><mi>γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Each fermion splits into two Majorana operators; at μ = 0, Δ = t, γ₁ and γ_{2N} drop out of the Hamiltonian."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>μ</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>k</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Bulk spectrum: the gap closes at μ = ±2t, the topological transition."}],"example":{"q":"In a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> sample a constriction backscatters a current <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> nA. Compute the shot-noise power for charge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and for charge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: how different are they?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>5.34</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.07</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>28</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> A²/Hz.","For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>28</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> A²/Hz, exactly three times larger. Noise this small can only be measured at mK temperatures with cryogenic amplifiers and long averaging."],"a":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.1</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>28</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> versus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>28</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> A²/Hz, a factor of three. The 1997 experiments measured the smaller one, showing that what crosses the constriction is a third of an electron."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-leinaas1977\" data-ref=\"leinaas1977\">Leinaas &amp; Myrheim 1977</a> · <a href=\"#ref-tsui1982\" data-ref=\"tsui1982\">Tsui, Stormer &amp; Gossard 1982</a> · <a href=\"#ref-wilczek1982\" data-ref=\"wilczek1982\">Wilczek 1982</a> · <a href=\"#ref-laughlin1983\" data-ref=\"laughlin1983\">Laughlin 1983</a> · <a href=\"#ref-depicciotto1997\" data-ref=\"depicciotto1997\">de-Picciotto et al. 1997</a> · <a href=\"#ref-saminadayar1997\" data-ref=\"saminadayar1997\">Saminadayar et al. 1997</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kitaev2001\" data-ref=\"kitaev2001\">Kitaev 2001</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kitaev2003\" data-ref=\"kitaev2003\">Kitaev 2003</a> · <a href=\"#ref-nayak2008\" data-ref=\"nayak2008\">Nayak et al. 2008</a> · <a href=\"#ref-mourik2012\" data-ref=\"mourik2012\">Mourik et al. 2012</a> · <a href=\"#ref-bartolomei2020\" data-ref=\"bartolomei2020\">Bartolomei et al. 2020</a> · <a href=\"#ref-nakamura2020\" data-ref=\"nakamura2020\">Nakamura et al. 2020</a> · <a href=\"#ref-aghaee2023\" data-ref=\"aghaee2023\">Aghaee et al. 2023</a>"},"measure":{"id":"measure","body":[{"p":"Almost every measurement rests on one idea: a probe goes in with energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and momentum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and comes out with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, handing the difference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> to the sample. In the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> plane, peaks in intensity trace the quasiparticle dispersion and their widths give lifetimes (<a href=\"#c-what\" data-open=\"what\">01 · What a quasiparticle is</a>). Different probes reach different ranges of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> and couple to different things in the sample:"},{"table":{"head":["Method","Couples to","Sees which quasiparticles"],"rows":[["Inelastic neutron scattering","nuclei (nuclear force) and magnetic moments","phonons and magnons across the whole Brillouin zone <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-brockhouse1955\" data-ref=\"brockhouse1955\">Brockhouse &amp; Stewart 1955</a>; <a href=\"#ref-brockhouse1957\" data-ref=\"brockhouse1957\">Brockhouse 1957</a>]</span>"],["Inelastic and resonant inelastic X-ray scattering","electron density (RIXS also spins and orbitals)","phonons, magnons, excitons; samples can be tiny"],["Raman and infrared spectroscopy","polarisability and dipole moment","zone-centre optical phonons and polaritons <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-henry1965\" data-ref=\"henry1965\">Henry &amp; Hopfield 1965</a>]</span>"],["Absorption and photoluminescence","optical transitions","excitons and exciton-polaritons"],["Electron energy-loss spectroscopy","charge density (Coulomb field)","bulk and surface plasmons <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-powell1959\" data-ref=\"powell1959\">Powell &amp; Swan 1959</a>]</span>"],["Tunnelling and scanning tunnelling spectroscopy","local density of states","superconducting gaps and Bogoliubov quasiparticles <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-giaever1960\" data-ref=\"giaever1960\">Giaever 1960</a>]</span>"],["Cyclotron resonance, quantum oscillations","carrier orbits in a magnetic field","effective masses and Fermi surfaces <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-dresselhaus1955\" data-ref=\"dresselhaus1955\">Dresselhaus, Kip &amp; Kittel 1955</a>]</span>"],["Shot noise, interferometry","charge and phase","fractional charge and anyon statistics (<a href=\"#c-anyons\" data-open=\"anyons\">11 · Anyons and Majoranas: quasiparticles that exist only in two dimensions</a>)"],["Angle-resolved photoemission","occupied electron states","the electron spectral function A(k, ω): dispersion, Z, lifetime, kinks"]],"cap":"Each method measures some response function (dynamic structure factor, susceptibility, spectral function); quasiparticles are peaks in these functions."}},{"h":"Angle-resolved photoemission"},{"p":"The photoelectric effect knocks electrons out of the sample surface. Measure the outgoing electron’s kinetic energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>kin</mtext></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: energy conservation gives its binding energy in the sample, and conservation of in-plane momentum gives its in-plane crystal momentum:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>kin</mtext></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>k</mi><mo>∥</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>kin</mtext></msub></mrow></msqrt><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi></mfrac><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.512</mn><mtext> </mtext><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>A</mtext><mo>˚</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>kin</mtext></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mtext>eV</mtext></mrow></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":""},{"p":"Under certain approximations (the “sudden approximation”, ignoring variations of the matrix elements) the measured intensity is proportional to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>: the spectral function times the Fermi function, i.e. the occupied half of the very quantity <a href=\"#c-what\" data-open=\"what\">01 · What a quasiparticle is</a> uses to judge quasiparticles <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-damascelli2003\" data-ref=\"damascelli2003\">Damascelli, Hussain &amp; Shen 2003</a>]</span>. So ARPES sees bands, Fermi surfaces and the widths and weights of quasiparticle peaks directly, and is a workhorse for cuprates, topological materials and two-dimensional materials <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-sobota2021\" data-ref=\"sobota2021\">Sobota, He &amp; Shen 2021</a>]</span>."},{"h":"Kinks: one quasiparticle’s mark on another"},{"p":"When electrons couple to a bosonic mode of energy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (a phonon, a magnetic excitation), the self-energy has a step at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>: within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> of the Fermi level an electron lacks the energy to emit a real phonon and can only travel “wearing” virtual ones, heavier and sharper; beyond it, the electron can emit real phonons and decay, returning to its original slope and broadening. The band bends into a <b>kink</b> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. The kink’s position gives the boson’s energy, and the ratio of slopes near and far from the Fermi level gives the coupling constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. In 2001 Lanzara and co-workers saw 50–80 meV kinks in several cuprates and attributed them to electron–phonon coupling <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-lanzara2001\" data-ref=\"lanzara2001\">Lanzara et al. 2001</a>]</span>; the kinks’ origin (phonons or magnetic excitations) was debated for a long time afterwards. The demo below computes what ARPES would see for an Einstein phonon."},{"demo":"arpes"},{"p":"This is one of the most beautiful scenes in the quasiparticle picture: a single image shows the electron quasiparticle (the spectral peak), its clothing (the mass increase and reduced <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi></mrow></semantics></math> below the kink), the other quasiparticle doing the dressing (the phonon’s energy), and the channel through which it decays (the broadening above the kink)."},{"note":"<b>Related sections.</b> The details of measuring phonons with neutrons, Raman, infrared and X-rays are in the <a href=\"../quasiparticles/phonons/#c-measure\">Phonon hall</a>; how to read spectra from 91 methods, including Raman, infrared and electron energy loss, is in the <a href=\"../spectra/\">Spectra hall</a>.","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"Energy and momentum transfer","p":"Peaks at the (q, ω) handed to the sample trace the dispersion; widths give lifetimes.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Different probes see different quasiparticles","p":"Neutrons see phonons and magnons, light sees optical phonons and excitons, electron energy loss sees plasmons.","st":"tested"},{"h":"ARPES measures the spectral function","p":"In the sudden approximation the intensity ∝ A(k, ω) f(ω); matrix elements and final-state effects modify it.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Kinks","p":"Bosonic modes leave kinks on bands, giving their energy ħω_E and the coupling λ.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Origin of the cuprate kinks","p":"Phonons or magnetic excitations: long debated.","st":"open"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>∝</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">q</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The scattering cross-section is proportional to the dynamic structure factor."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∝</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"ARPES intensity (sudden approximation)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">∣</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">∣</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The self-energy for coupling to one Einstein phonon at T = 0."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi><mtext>bare</mtext></msubsup><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>F</mi><mtext>renorm</mtext></msubsup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mi>ω</mi></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ</mi><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">∣</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Reading λ off the velocity near the Fermi level."}],"example":{"q":"ARPES with a 21.2 eV helium lamp on a sample with a 4.5 eV work function. What is the kinetic energy of electrons emitted from the Fermi level? What is their in-plane momentum at a 10° emission angle? The measured dispersion has a slope of 1.5 eV·Å near the Fermi level and 2.7 eV·Å below 100 meV: what is λ?","steps":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mtext>kin</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>21.2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>4.5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>16.7</mn></mrow></semantics></math> eV. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mo>∥</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.512</mn><msqrt><mn>16.7</mn></msqrt><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>10</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.512</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>4.09</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>0.174</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.364</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Å⁻¹.","The slope far away approximates the bare velocity, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.7</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1.5</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.8</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow></semantics></math>."],"a":"16.7 eV and 0.36 Å⁻¹ (a typical Brillouin zone is about 1.6 Å⁻¹ wide, so a few tens of degrees sweep most of it); <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, quite strong coupling. A real analysis must also allow for curvature of the “bare band” itself, so this is only an estimate."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-brockhouse1955\" data-ref=\"brockhouse1955\">Brockhouse &amp; Stewart 1955</a> · <a href=\"#ref-dresselhaus1955\" data-ref=\"dresselhaus1955\">Dresselhaus, Kip &amp; Kittel 1955</a> · <a href=\"#ref-brockhouse1957\" data-ref=\"brockhouse1957\">Brockhouse 1957</a> · <a href=\"#ref-powell1959\" data-ref=\"powell1959\">Powell &amp; Swan 1959</a> · <a href=\"#ref-giaever1960\" data-ref=\"giaever1960\">Giaever 1960</a> · <a href=\"#ref-henry1965\" data-ref=\"henry1965\">Henry &amp; Hopfield 1965</a> · <a href=\"#ref-lanzara2001\" data-ref=\"lanzara2001\">Lanzara et al. 2001</a> · <a href=\"#ref-damascelli2003\" data-ref=\"damascelli2003\">Damascelli, Hussain &amp; Shen 2003</a> · <a href=\"#ref-sobota2021\" data-ref=\"sobota2021\">Sobota, He &amp; Shen 2021</a>"}}};
